{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2d8GApV7kx2NqXXtHf","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GLh14BX7RusR8DNYutNoih","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":180},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":810,"cache_read_input_tokens":420465,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":810},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"cba17acc-a954-4d45-9625-213508d146d2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:34:59.647Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2d81YTEB3Rd4St8HrC","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01GLh14BX7RusR8DNYutNoih":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":180}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GLh14BX7RusR8DNYutNoih","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Batzooka Baseball\n2\t\n3\t*The build plan for the over-the-top arcade baseball demo that TASK.md asks for. Read TASK.md first. This plan settles\n4\teverything TASK.md leaves open: the title, genre, cameras, architecture, every system, the content, the build order, the\n5\trisks and the tests. A capable developer (or model) should be able to build the whole demo from TASK.md and this file\n6\talone, without the planner.*\n7\t\n8\t**Title: BATZOOKA BASEBALL.** Picked the way TASK.md asks. Fifty playful riffs on \"baseball\" were brainstormed and\n9\tnumbered without being shown, then a real RNG drew the number (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`\n10\tprinted **37**: *Batzooka Baseball*). The name also gives the game its signature mechanic: the **Batzooka**, a\n11\trocket-powered bat that the crowd's roar charges up.\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## 0. At a glance\n16\t\n17\t| | |\n18\t|---|---|\n19\t| Game | **Batzooka Baseball**, an original over-the-top arcade baseball game |\n20\t| Pitch | Pitch fireballs, swing a rocket-powered bat, rob homers by climbing the wall of a moon dome, and empty the benches in slapstick dust clouds, on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark from a mega-corporation. |\n21\t| Core loop | Win the duel at the plate (pitch or swing on timing and aim), then survive the chaos after contact (field, throw, run, slide), while big plays charge the Batzooka meter that unlocks supers. |\n22\t| Genre | Arcade sports (baseball), plus a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light upgrades |\n23\t| Perspective | **Plate Cam**: a pseudo-3D Mode 7 view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing. **Broadcast Cam**: a three-quarter view of the park for fielding and baserunning. **Moonshot Cam**: a Mode 7 chase camera for home runs. Replays use a turned view. |\n24\t| Players | 1–4 on one machine (versus or co-op); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (a ghost-mode Derby round) |\n25\t| Modes | Tour (story: 5 road games and a boss), Exhibition, Batzooka Derby (home-run derby), Spring Training (tutorial); plus attract mode and dev mode |\n26\t| Engine | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, every module), with the small documented changes in §3.3 |\n27\t| Look | The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; textured TileMap ballparks; lit night games; particles everywhere |\n28\t| Audio | The engine's chip synth only: original music, sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices. No audio files. |\n29\t\n30\t**Exact deliverable names.** In file names, TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]` is written with GameTitle = `BatzookaBaseball` and SOURCE = `Baseball`:\n31\t\n32\t- dev folder: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/` (inside this folder)\n33\t- the game, a single file: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819.html` (in this folder, beside PLAN.md)\n34\t- `exit-interview.json` (in this folder, beside the .html)\n35\t\n36\t**Traceability.** Where each TASK.md requirement is handled:\n37\t\n38\t| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Genre and perspective | §2, §6.12 |\n41\t| Title by RNG | top of this file |\n42\t| Single HTML package | §12 |\n43\t| Built on the handoff engine; engine may change | §3, §4 |\n44\t| Art direction (engine style plus new baseball assets) | §8 |\n45\t| Animation (engine rigs plus new baseball motion) | §8.3 |\n46\t| Audio in code; Animalese voices | §6.15, §6.16 |\n47\t| Online: AI Hub `lib/hub` | §6.21 |\n48\t| Multiplayer on its own | §6.20 |\n49\t| Dev folder, bundling, shared machine (ports, processes) | §4.1, §11.1, §12 |\n50\t| Feature-complete vertical slice; infrastructure for the rest | §1, §6, §7, §9 |\n51\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §6.23 |\n52\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle, any input exits | §6.22 |\n53\t| Narrative tone in the source's style | §6.15, §7.5, §7.9 |\n54\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6.13, §6.14, §6.24 |\n55\t| Touch-only devices | §6.26 |\n56\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §12 |\n57\t| Source Information and feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | §6.27, §12 |\n58\t| exit-interview.json | §6.27, §12 |\n59\t| Testing: headless, one command, scripted playthrough | §11 |\n60\t\n61\t---\n62\t\n63\t## 1. Design pillars\n64\t\n65\t1. **The duel at the plate.** Baseball is a duel of timing and guesswork. Every pitch is seen from the Plate Cam: the\n66\t   pitcher picks a pitch and spot and times the release, then steers the ball a little in flight. The batter reads it,\n67\t   aims the sweet spot and times the swing. The result comes straight from timing and aim, so it is readable and fair.\n68\t2. **Chaos after contact.** At the crack of the bat the camera cuts wide. The ball becomes the star: fielders chase its\n69\t   shadow, dive, climb walls and fire throws at the bases, while runners turbo, slide and bowl over catchers.\n70\t3. **Baseball's texture.** The count, the diamond, the chalk lines, the organ, the PA voice echoing names, the crowd's\n71\t   roar and groan, the play-by-play duo, vendors, mascots, the between-innings show. It must feel like a day (or a\n72\t   night) at the ballpark.\n73\t4. **Over the top.** Every system has a turned-to-eleven version: pitches that catch fire, vanish or zig-zag; a bat\n74\t   that fires the ball like a rocket; fielders who climb walls and throw lasers; benches that empty into cartoon dust\n75\t   clouds; parks on a volcano, by the sea, on the moon, in a graveyard and in a corporate megadome.\n76\t5. **Ten feet away, one controller.** Big, bold and few things on screen at once. Every prompt shows the button on the\n77\t   device used last. Keyboard and mouse feel native: the mouse aims the bat and the pitch and clicks bases to throw.\n78\t6. **Readable rules.** Real baseball where it is fun, arcade-simple where it is fussy. §6.1 lists the rules kept,\n79\t   simplified and dropped, and Spring Training teaches what the player can and cannot do.\n80\t\n81\t**Tone.** The voice of old-time radio play-by-play turned up to arcade volume: tall tales, baseball slang, warm\n82\thyperbole and running gags. It is never mean, and its violence is cartoon slapstick (§6.15).\n83\t\n84\t---\n85\t\n86\t## 2. Genre and perspective\n87\t\n88\t**Genre: arcade baseball.** The source is baseball \"over the top, arcade style\", so the game is a full arcade baseball\n89\tgame: real innings, counts, outs and runs, with exaggerated physics, super moves and a meter. Two other genres are woven\n90\tin to carry the over-the-top tone:\n91\t- a **brawler mini-game** (the Rhubarb), a 12-second bench-clearing scrum built on the engine's beat-'em-up tools;\n92\t- a **story Tour** with light RPG progression (Fan Bucks, team upgrades, rewards from beaten rivals), which gives the\n93\t  demo a beginning, a middle, an end and a boss.\n94\t\n95\t**Perspective: two main cameras over one world.** Every great arcade baseball game splits the screen's job: the duel\n96\tis seen from behind the plate, the play after contact from up high. Both cameras draw the same world (the same TileMap\n97\tand world coordinates), so cutting between them is seamless.\n98\t\n99\t| Camera | When | Built with | Why |\n100\t|---|---|---|---|\n101\t| **Plate Cam** | every pitch, swing, bunt, steal start and pickoff | `E.Mode7` (the mode7 module): a perspective floor (the park's own TileMap floor), the park's Backdrop as the sky, rigs as billboards scaled by distance, and walls, stands and the ball drawn with `m7.add` | The iconic baseball framing. The pitch grows as it comes in, which is what makes timing a swing feel right. Mode 7 is the engine's native perspective view. |\n102\t| **Broadcast Cam** | ball in play: fielding, throws, baserunning, rundowns, the Rhubarb, cutscenes | a custom ground view `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 52, 1, 1)` over the park TileMap; `cam.frame` on the ball, its landing marker and the chaser | The engine's richest look (lit TileMap, shadows, props, particles, crowd). Home plate sits at the bottom of the screen, so up on the stick is toward center field. |\n103\t| **Moonshot Cam** | home runs and Batzooka blasts | Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball)` in slow motion, then a stands shot and the trot in Broadcast | The spectacle beat |\n104\t| **Replay Cam** | after big plays (optional, skippable) | Broadcast data replayed in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45) at half speed | A second angle on the highlight |\n105\t\n106\tThe Broadcast Cam also has the player options **High** (`topdown`, pitch 70) and **Isometric** (`iso`), in Settings\n107\tand on R3 / V.\n108\t\n109\t---\n110\t\n111\t## 3. Tech stack and engine\n112\t\n113\t### 3.1 What to fetch (once, in M0)\n114\t\n115\tCopy everything into the dev folder; never edit anything outside it.\n116\t\n117\t- **The engine handoff package**, from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`: a shallow\n118\t  clone into its own temporary folder (or raw files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`).\n119\t  Take `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, every module), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the\n120\t  `tools/` folder (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find, asset-lib, asset-set), `package.json` (pins\n121\t  Playwright 1.63.0), and `kits/sports.js` and `kits/kart.js` to read (ball and formation code; Mode 7 rigs).\n122\t- **The motion clips**, from the same repository (they are not in the handoff): `src/mocap/readable.js`,\n123\t  `src/mocap/mocap.js`, `tools/anim-set.mjs` with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`, `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_124.js` (CMU subject\n124\t  124: *Baseball Pitch* 124_01 and 124_02, *Baseball Swing* 124_07, *Baseball Bunt* 124_08, *Underhand Toss* 124_12,\n125\t  *Underhand Fast Pitch* 124_13, *2 Foot Jump* 124_11), `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js` (`Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n126\t  `Slide_Exit`, `Punch_Jab`, `Punch_Cross`), `src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js` (`Victory`, `Cheering_Two_Hands`,\n127\t  `Cheer_One_arm`), and `docs/MOCAP.md` (read its usage parts).\n128\t- **The AI Hub client and harness**: only these files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`:\n129\t  `README.md` (the protocol), `session.js` (client 1.4.0; pasted **unmodified**), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n130\t  `round.js`. Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is over 5 GB.\n131\t\n132\t### 3.2 Engine features to use (don't reinvent them)\n133\t\n134\t`new E.Game({ res: 'ps1', expand: true, menu: {...} })` · scenes with `pausable`, `view`, `input`, `camera`, `touch` ·\n135\tboot steps (`game.start({ boot })`) · `E.TileMap` (floor tags, `floorTex`, wall `types` with `face`, `roof`, `cut`) ·\n136\t`E.Body` for players · `E.Humanoid` (builds, outfits, hats, `colors`, poses, `expr`, `attack` specs, `drawPortrait`) ·\n137\t`E.Blob` for mascots, gulls and ghosts · `E.Mode7` · `E.Backdrop` · `r.shape` / `E.SHAPES` / `map.solid` · `r.prop`\n138\t(lamps, flags, banners, torches, barrels) · `game.lights` · `game.particles` and `game.hitFx` · `game.weather` (ash,\n139\tleaves, rain) · `game.flash`, `shake`, `freeze`, `timeScale` · `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path` (blimp, drones,\n140\tgulls, camera moves) · `E.arc` (throws) · `E.rng` (seeded streams) · `E.Dialog` (cutscenes, radio chatter, portraits) ·\n141\t`E.Menu` · `E.Arcade` (high-score tables and the initials letter wheel) · `game.saves` · `game.settings` and\n142\t`settings.define` (sliders, selects, toggles) · `game.input` (custom preset, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`,\n143\t`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`, `halfKeys`, `rumble`, `suppressCode`, `clicked`, `pointer`, `mouseGround`) · `game.audio`\n144\t(`define`, `sfx`, `music` with named layers, `sting`, `tempo`, `duck`) · `E.Combo`, `E.Attack`, `E.knockback` (the\n145\tRhubarb) · `E.font.title`, `E.ui.box`, `E.ui.bar` · `game.debug`. Motion clips go through `Mocap.load`, `Mocap.drive`,\n146\t`lib.sample`, `rig.mocapW`, `rig.mocapMask` and `MocapReadable.mirror`.\n147\t\n148\t**Already built in; don't rebuild:** the pause menu, the settings screens, control rebinding (with conflict checks),\n149\tbutton-symbol families, full screen, the corner menu button, pausing when focus is lost or a pad is unplugged, the boot\n150\tscreen and crash report, save slots, the comfort settings (reduce motion, shake, flashes) and screen filters (CRT and\n151\tscanlines suit an arcade game).\n152\t\n153\t### 3.3 Engine changes (in the dev folder's copy; list each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and in the About screen's engine feedback)\n154\t\n155\t1. **`weapon: 'bat'`** on `E.Humanoid`: add it to the built-in list (the warning at `this.o.weapon && ![...]`), a rest\n156\t   direction (bat up over the back shoulder), a draw branch beside `'staff'`, and `tip()` = the barrel's end. The bat is\n157\t   a tapered capsule: knob, thin handle, thick barrel, in `colors.bat` (ash `#c89a5a`; the Batzooka is drawn by the\n158\t   game over it). With `grip: 'both'`, IK the left hand to the handle 2.5 U below the right after every\n159\t   `solvePose()`, so swings and bunts hold the bat in two hands.\n160\t2. **`mitt`** option: `'fielder' | 'catcher' | 'first' | null`, a leather mitt on the glove hand (`handL` for\n161\t   right-handed throwers, `handR` for lefties): a pocketed oval about 2.2× the hand's size, in `colors.mitt`. The\n162\t   catcher's mitt is rounder and bigger.\n163\t3. **Hats:** `hat: 'batting'` (a glossy helmet with a brim and an ear flap on the side facing the pitcher), `hat: 'mask'`\n164\t   (a catcher's helmet with a cage of 3 bars over the face), and `hat: 'visor'` (a space helmet for the moon team: a\n165\t   dome with a tinted front), and `hat: 'tophat'` (Mortimer Megamax's tall silk hat). Portraits draw them too.\n166\t4. **Font:** add the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font, so any `input.label()` text renders (the in-game prompts still use\n167\t   the icon renderer, §6.13).\n168\t5. **The standard `glyphs` setting** (group Controller, \"Button symbols\"): default `'playstation'`; options\n169\t   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation (✕ ○ □ △)'], ['xbox', 'Xbox (A B X Y)']]`. A DualSense or any standard pad then shows\n170\t   PlayStation symbols unless the player picks Xbox.\n171\t6. Only if Mode 7 needs it: a helper `m7.quad(pts4, color, depth)` that draws a filled world-space quad sorted with\n172\t   the billboards (the fence and the stand faces). It can live in game code over `m7.add` instead.\n173\t7. Fix any engine bug the tests uncover, with a line on what and why.\n174\t\n175\tKeep every change small and commented `// BATZOOKA:`. Nothing in the game may depend on code outside the dev folder.\n176\t\n177\t---\n178\t\n179\t## 4. Architecture\n180\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"375fe95f-06bd-4a46-884c-9344d94c6b0d","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:34:59.658Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Batzooka Baseball\n\n*The build plan for the over-the-top arcade baseball demo that TASK.md asks for. Read TASK.md first. This plan settles\neverything TASK.md leaves open: the title, genre, cameras, architecture, every system, the content, the build order, the\nrisks and the tests. A capable developer (or model) should be able to build the whole demo from TASK.md and this file\nalone, without the planner.*\n\n**Title: BATZOOKA BASEBALL.** Picked the way TASK.md asks. Fifty playful riffs on \"baseball\" were brainstormed and\nnumbered without being shown, then a real RNG drew the number (`python3 -c \"import secrets; print(secrets.randbelow(50) + 1)\"`\nprinted **37**: *Batzooka Baseball*). The name also gives the game its signature mechanic: the **Batzooka**, a\nrocket-powered bat that the crowd's roar charges up.\n\n---\n\n## 0. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Game | **Batzooka Baseball**, an original over-the-top arcade baseball game |\n| Pitch | Pitch fireballs, swing a rocket-powered bat, rob homers by climbing the wall of a moon dome, and empty the benches in slapstick dust clouds, on a barnstorming tour to save a cornfield ballpark from a mega-corporation. |\n| Core loop | Win the duel at the plate (pitch or swing on timing and aim), then survive the chaos after contact (field, throw, run, slide), while big plays charge the Batzooka meter that unlocks supers. |\n| Genre | Arcade sports (baseball), plus a slapstick brawler mini-game (the Rhubarb) and a short story Tour with light upgrades |\n| Perspective | **Plate Cam**: a pseudo-3D Mode 7 view from behind the catcher for every pitch and swing. **Broadcast Cam**: a three-quarter view of the park for fielding and baserunning. **Moonshot Cam**: a Mode 7 chase camera for home runs. Replays use a turned view. |\n| Players | 1–4 on one machine (versus or co-op); 1–4 online in AI Hub sessions (a ghost-mode Derby round) |\n| Modes | Tour (story: 5 road games and a boss), Exhibition, Batzooka Derby (home-run derby), Spring Training (tutorial); plus attract mode and dev mode |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge v0.85.2 handoff package, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, every module), with the small documented changes in §3.3 |\n| Look | The engine's native look: HD Humanoid rigs drawn as pixel art at `res: 'ps1'` with `expand: true`; textured TileMap ballparks; lit night games; particles everywhere |\n| Audio | The engine's chip synth only: original music, sound effects and Animalese-style babble voices. No audio files. |\n\n**Exact deliverable names.** In file names, TASK.md's `[GameTitle-SOURCE-…]` is written with GameTitle = `BatzookaBaseball` and SOURCE = `Baseball`:\n\n- dev folder: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/` (inside this folder)\n- the game, a single file: `BatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819.html` (in this folder, beside PLAN.md)\n- `exit-interview.json` (in this folder, beside the .html)\n\n**Traceability.** Where each TASK.md requirement is handled:\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Genre and perspective | §2, §6.12 |\n| Title by RNG | top of this file |\n| Single HTML package | §12 |\n| Built on the handoff engine; engine may change | §3, §4 |\n| Art direction (engine style plus new baseball assets) | §8 |\n| Animation (engine rigs plus new baseball motion) | §8.3 |\n| Audio in code; Animalese voices | §6.15, §6.16 |\n| Online: AI Hub `lib/hub` | §6.21 |\n| Multiplayer on its own | §6.20 |\n| Dev folder, bundling, shared machine (ports, processes) | §4.1, §11.1, §12 |\n| Feature-complete vertical slice; infrastructure for the rest | §1, §6, §7, §9 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §6.23 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle, any input exits | §6.22 |\n| Narrative tone in the source's style | §6.15, §7.5, §7.9 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6.13, §6.14, §6.24 |\n| Touch-only devices | §6.26 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §12 |\n| Source Information and feedback in the chat reply and an About/Credits screen | §6.27, §12 |\n| exit-interview.json | §6.27, §12 |\n| Testing: headless, one command, scripted playthrough | §11 |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Design pillars\n\n1. **The duel at the plate.** Baseball is a duel of timing and guesswork. Every pitch is seen from the Plate Cam: the\n   pitcher picks a pitch and spot and times the release, then steers the ball a little in flight. The batter reads it,\n   aims the sweet spot and times the swing. The result comes straight from timing and aim, so it is readable and fair.\n2. **Chaos after contact.** At the crack of the bat the camera cuts wide. The ball becomes the star: fielders chase its\n   shadow, dive, climb walls and fire throws at the bases, while runners turbo, slide and bowl over catchers.\n3. **Baseball's texture.** The count, the diamond, the chalk lines, the organ, the PA voice echoing names, the crowd's\n   roar and groan, the play-by-play duo, vendors, mascots, the between-innings show. It must feel like a day (or a\n   night) at the ballpark.\n4. **Over the top.** Every system has a turned-to-eleven version: pitches that catch fire, vanish or zig-zag; a bat\n   that fires the ball like a rocket; fielders who climb walls and throw lasers; benches that empty into cartoon dust\n   clouds; parks on a volcano, by the sea, on the moon, in a graveyard and in a corporate megadome.\n5. **Ten feet away, one controller.** Big, bold and few things on screen at once. Every prompt shows the button on the\n   device used last. Keyboard and mouse feel native: the mouse aims the bat and the pitch and clicks bases to throw.\n6. **Readable rules.** Real baseball where it is fun, arcade-simple where it is fussy. §6.1 lists the rules kept,\n   simplified and dropped, and Spring Training teaches what the player can and cannot do.\n\n**Tone.** The voice of old-time radio play-by-play turned up to arcade volume: tall tales, baseball slang, warm\nhyperbole and running gags. It is never mean, and its violence is cartoon slapstick (§6.15).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Genre and perspective\n\n**Genre: arcade baseball.** The source is baseball \"over the top, arcade style\", so the game is a full arcade baseball\ngame: real innings, counts, outs and runs, with exaggerated physics, super moves and a meter. Two other genres are woven\nin to carry the over-the-top tone:\n- a **brawler mini-game** (the Rhubarb), a 12-second bench-clearing scrum built on the engine's beat-'em-up tools;\n- a **story Tour** with light RPG progression (Fan Bucks, team upgrades, rewards from beaten rivals), which gives the\n  demo a beginning, a middle, an end and a boss.\n\n**Perspective: two main cameras over one world.** Every great arcade baseball game splits the screen's job: the duel\nis seen from behind the plate, the play after contact from up high. Both cameras draw the same world (the same TileMap\nand world coordinates), so cutting between them is seamless.\n\n| Camera | When | Built with | Why |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Plate Cam** | every pitch, swing, bunt, steal start and pickoff | `E.Mode7` (the mode7 module): a perspective floor (the park's own TileMap floor), the park's Backdrop as the sky, rigs as billboards scaled by distance, and walls, stands and the ball drawn with `m7.add` | The iconic baseball framing. The pitch grows as it comes in, which is what makes timing a swing feel right. Mode 7 is the engine's native perspective view. |\n| **Broadcast Cam** | ball in play: fielding, throws, baserunning, rundowns, the Rhubarb, cutscenes | a custom ground view `new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 52, 1, 1)` over the park TileMap; `cam.frame` on the ball, its landing marker and the chaser | The engine's richest look (lit TileMap, shadows, props, particles, crowd). Home plate sits at the bottom of the screen, so up on the stick is toward center field. |\n| **Moonshot Cam** | home runs and Batzooka blasts | Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball)` in slow motion, then a stands shot and the trot in Broadcast | The spectacle beat |\n| **Replay Cam** | after big plays (optional, skippable) | Broadcast data replayed in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45) at half speed | A second angle on the highlight |\n\nThe Broadcast Cam also has the player options **High** (`topdown`, pitch 70) and **Isometric** (`iso`), in Settings\nand on R3 / V.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and engine\n\n### 3.1 What to fetch (once, in M0)\n\nCopy everything into the dev folder; never edit anything outside it.\n\n- **The engine handoff package**, from `https://github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`: a shallow\n  clone into its own temporary folder (or raw files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/main/<path>`).\n  Take `my-3d2dge.js` (the full engine, every module), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `docs/ASSETS.md`, the\n  `tools/` folder (check, filmstrip, replay, asset-sheet, asset-find, asset-lib, asset-set), `package.json` (pins\n  Playwright 1.63.0), and `kits/sports.js` and `kits/kart.js` to read (ball and formation code; Mode 7 rigs).\n- **The motion clips**, from the same repository (they are not in the handoff): `src/mocap/readable.js`,\n  `src/mocap/mocap.js`, `tools/anim-set.mjs` with `tools/mocap-lib.mjs`, `examples/cmu-lib/CMU_124.js` (CMU subject\n  124: *Baseball Pitch* 124_01 and 124_02, *Baseball Swing* 124_07, *Baseball Bunt* 124_08, *Underhand Toss* 124_12,\n  *Underhand Fast Pitch* 124_13, *2 Foot Jump* 124_11), `src/mocap/sets/quaternius.js` (`Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n  `Slide_Exit`, `Punch_Jab`, `Punch_Cross`), `src/mocap/sets/mesh2motion.js` (`Victory`, `Cheering_Two_Hands`,\n  `Cheer_One_arm`), and `docs/MOCAP.md` (read its usage parts).\n- **The AI Hub client and harness**: only these files from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/`:\n  `README.md` (the protocol), `session.js` (client 1.4.0; pasted **unmodified**), `harness.html`, `bridge.js`,\n  `round.js`. Do not clone the ai-hub repository: it is over 5 GB.\n\n### 3.2 Engine features to use (don't reinvent them)\n\n`new E.Game({ res: 'ps1', expand: true, menu: {...} })` · scenes with `pausable`, `view`, `input`, `camera`, `touch` ·\nboot steps (`game.start({ boot })`) · `E.TileMap` (floor tags, `floorTex`, wall `types` with `face`, `roof`, `cut`) ·\n`E.Body` for players · `E.Humanoid` (builds, outfits, hats, `colors`, poses, `expr`, `attack` specs, `drawPortrait`) ·\n`E.Blob` for mascots, gulls and ghosts · `E.Mode7` · `E.Backdrop` · `r.shape` / `E.SHAPES` / `map.solid` · `r.prop`\n(lamps, flags, banners, torches, barrels) · `game.lights` · `game.particles` and `game.hitFx` · `game.weather` (ash,\nleaves, rain) · `game.flash`, `shake`, `freeze`, `timeScale` · `game.tween`, `game.script`, `E.Path` (blimp, drones,\ngulls, camera moves) · `E.arc` (throws) · `E.rng` (seeded streams) · `E.Dialog` (cutscenes, radio chatter, portraits) ·\n`E.Menu` · `E.Arcade` (high-score tables and the initials letter wheel) · `game.saves` · `game.settings` and\n`settings.define` (sliders, selects, toggles) · `game.input` (custom preset, `label`, `glyphs`, `lastDevice`,\n`allowJoin`, `addPlayer`, `halfKeys`, `rumble`, `suppressCode`, `clicked`, `pointer`, `mouseGround`) · `game.audio`\n(`define`, `sfx`, `music` with named layers, `sting`, `tempo`, `duck`) · `E.Combo`, `E.Attack`, `E.knockback` (the\nRhubarb) · `E.font.title`, `E.ui.box`, `E.ui.bar` · `game.debug`. Motion clips go through `Mocap.load`, `Mocap.drive`,\n`lib.sample`, `rig.mocapW`, `rig.mocapMask` and `MocapReadable.mirror`.\n\n**Already built in; don't rebuild:** the pause menu, the settings screens, control rebinding (with conflict checks),\nbutton-symbol families, full screen, the corner menu button, pausing when focus is lost or a pad is unplugged, the boot\nscreen and crash report, save slots, the comfort settings (reduce motion, shake, flashes) and screen filters (CRT and\nscanlines suit an arcade game).\n\n### 3.3 Engine changes (in the dev folder's copy; list each in `ENGINE-CHANGES.md` and in the About screen's engine feedback)\n\n1. **`weapon: 'bat'`** on `E.Humanoid`: add it to the built-in list (the warning at `this.o.weapon && ![...]`), a rest\n   direction (bat up over the back shoulder), a draw branch beside `'staff'`, and `tip()` = the barrel's end. The bat is\n   a tapered capsule: knob, thin handle, thick barrel, in `colors.bat` (ash `#c89a5a`; the Batzooka is drawn by the\n   game over it). With `grip: 'both'`, IK the left hand to the handle 2.5 U below the right after every\n   `solvePose()`, so swings and bunts hold the bat in two hands.\n2. **`mitt`** option: `'fielder' | 'catcher' | 'first' | null`, a leather mitt on the glove hand (`handL` for\n   right-handed throwers, `handR` for lefties): a pocketed oval about 2.2× the hand's size, in `colors.mitt`. The\n   catcher's mitt is rounder and bigger.\n3. **Hats:** `hat: 'batting'` (a glossy helmet with a brim and an ear flap on the side facing the pitcher), `hat: 'mask'`\n   (a catcher's helmet with a cage of 3 bars over the face), and `hat: 'visor'` (a space helmet for the moon team: a\n   dome with a tinted front), and `hat: 'tophat'` (Mortimer Megamax's tall silk hat). Portraits draw them too.\n4. **Font:** add the glyphs ✕ ○ □ △ to the 5×7 font, so any `input.label()` text renders (the in-game prompts still use\n   the icon renderer, §6.13).\n5. **The standard `glyphs` setting** (group Controller, \"Button symbols\"): default `'playstation'`; options\n   `[['playstation', 'PlayStation (✕ ○ □ △)'], ['xbox', 'Xbox (A B X Y)']]`. A DualSense or any standard pad then shows\n   PlayStation symbols unless the player picks Xbox.\n6. Only if Mode 7 needs it: a helper `m7.quad(pts4, color, depth)` that draws a filled world-space quad sorted with\n   the billboards (the fence and the stand faces). It can live in game code over `m7.add` instead.\n7. Fix any engine bug the tests uncover, with a line on what and why.\n\nKeep every change small and commented `// BATZOOKA:`. Nothing in the game may depend on code outside the dev folder.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n","numLines":180,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2111}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GLh14BX7RusR8DNYutNoih","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
