{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01U3KJ4itkCSLQX1dsGiBT6c","type":"tool_result","content":"180\t\n181\t### 4.1 Dev folder layout\n182\t\n183\t```\n184\tBatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/\n185\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js          the engine: handoff v0.85.2 plus the changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n186\t  engine/mocap-readable.js     the repo's src/mocap/readable.js (unchanged)\n187\t  engine/mocap.js              the repo's src/mocap/mocap.js (unchanged)\n188\t  assets/mocap-batzooka.js     the clip set cut by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n189\t  vendor/aihub-session.js      AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n190\t  src/*.js                     the game: files concatenated by name into one IIFE (§4.2)\n191\t  build/game.js                the concatenated game (written by tools/build.mjs; dev page and tests load it)\n192\t  index.html                   dev page: engine, mocap, vendor client, build/game.js as separate scripts\n193\t  tools/build.mjs              src → build/game.js; then the single-file bundle → ../<name>.html\n194\t  tools/serve.mjs              a static server on 127.0.0.1, port 0 (prints the port; for the hub harness)\n195\t  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-lib.mjs, asset-set.mjs (handoff)\n196\t  tools/anim-set.mjs, mocap-lib.mjs (repo)\n197\t  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (AI Hub)\n198\t  tests/run.mjs                the one command (§11.1)\n199\t  tests/lib/*.mjs              the headless loader, assertions, bots\n200\t  tests/*.test.mjs             the tests (§11.3)\n201\t  package.json                 { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { test, build, check } }\n202\t  ENGINE-CHANGES.md            each engine change and why\n203\t  NOTES.md                     tuning notes, decisions, the feedback answers as they come\n204\t```\n205\t\n206\t### 4.2 Source files\n207\t\n208\t`tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside one `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();`.\n209\tEach file adds to one namespace object, `BZ`. Files never reach into each other's locals.\n210\t\n211\t| File | Holds |\n212\t|---|---|\n213\t| `00-core.js` | `BZ`; `BZ.def(kind, id, spec)` and `BZ.get(kind, id)` registries; the event bus `BZ.bus` (`on`, `off`, `emit`, a ring buffer of the last 200 events); RNG streams `BZ.rng.play / fx / deal`; `BZ.TUNE`, `BZ.TUNE_META`, `BZ.resetTune()`; unit helpers (`feet(u)`, `mph(uPerS)`, angle helpers); constants |\n214\t| `01-input.js` | the action preset; input contexts and routing; device tracking; the glyph icon renderer and prompt builder; mouse helpers (pointer → strike-zone plane, pointer → ground → base); touch-only detection |\n215\t| `02-audio.js` | SFX definitions; the crowd bed; the music director (song per scene, layers, stings, tempo); the **Babble** voice synth |\n216\t| `03-rigs.js` | player rig factory (team uniform + player looks); custom poses and attack specs (swing, bunt, windup, throw, tag, dive, climb, umpire calls); the mocap driver (clip windows, mirrored clips for lefties, blending); Batzooka and mitt overdraw |\n217\t| `04-shapes.js` | `E.SHAPES` entries for ballpark objects (§7.12) |\n218\t| `05-park.js` | the park builder: geometry from a park def → TileMap rows, legend, types and `floorTex`; fence function, foul lines and bases; stand seats; light rigs; props; the Plate Cam's Mode 7 floor, sky and world billboards |\n219\t| `10-rules.js` | the rules engine, pure logic: count, outs, bases, forces, scoring, half-innings, game end, extra innings, mercy rule; line score and box stats |\n220\t| `11-pitch.js` | pitch catalog; selection; windup and release ring; flight paths; aftertouch; stamina; calls; the catcher's catch and passed balls |\n221\t| `12-bat.js` | the sweet-spot reticle; swing types; the contact model; bunts; contact → launch |\n222\t| `13-ball.js` | batted and thrown ball physics; prediction; fence, pole, stands and foul tests; home runs; ground-rule doubles; wind |\n223\t| `14-field.js` | fielders: positions, roles, assignment, chase, catch, dive, jump, wall climb, throws, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns |\n224\t| `15-run.js` | baserunners: the batter-runner, forces, tag-ups, advance and retreat orders, steals, leads, pickoffs, slides, bowl-overs, scoring |\n225\t| `16-supers.js` | the Batzooka meter, the supers (pitch, bat, throw, run), turbo |\n226\t| `17-rhubarb.js` | the bench-clearing brawl |\n227\t| `18-hazards.js` | the six parks' hazards and features |\n228\t| `20-ai.js` | CPU pitcher, batter, fielders and runners by difficulty; **the bot**, which turns a CPU brain's choices into player inputs (for tests, the playthrough and the attract mode) |\n229\t| `21-cams.js` | Plate Cam, Broadcast Cam, Moonshot Cam, cut-ins, the replay ring buffer, transitions |\n230\t| `22-hud.js` | scorebug, count, bases, meters, player cards, prompts, call stamps, radar, radio box, pitch and timing feedback |\n231\t| `23-talk.js` | the commentary director (line pools, priorities, rate limit, variables), the PA and the umpire; the cutscene runner |\n232\t| `24-crowd.js` | crowd seats and animation; hype; the wave; the jumbotron; the mascot; fireworks; between-innings shows |\n233\t| `30-match.js` | the match scene: its phases, update order and draw order |\n234\t| `31-derby.js` | the Derby scene (local hot-seat and hub) |\n235\t| `32-training.js` | Spring Training lessons |\n236\t| `33-tour.js` | Tour map, clubhouse, story flow, rewards, saves |\n237\t| `34-menus.js` | title, mode select, team select and joining, results, Playbook (help), About/Credits, pause items |\n238\t| `35-attract.js` | the attract director and its segments |\n239\t| `36-dev.js` | the dev mode overlay |\n240\t| `40-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow, its card, ghosts |\n241\t| `50-teams.js` … `56-songs.js` | content data: teams, parks, story, lessons, lines, upgrades, songs |\n242\t| `90-start.js` | boot steps, the scenes table, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n243\t\n244\t### 4.3 The spine (rules every module follows)\n245\t\n246\t- **One match state, `BZ.M`** (fields in §6.1). The simulation runs only in `update(dt)` (exact 1/120 s steps); `draw`\n247\t  only reads state. A whole match can run headless with `draw` skipped.\n248\t- **Seeded gameplay.** Gameplay randomness comes only from `BZ.rng.play` (`E.rng(seed)`, re-seeded per match;\n249\t  `game.debug.setup({ seed })`). The Derby's pitch sequence comes from `BZ.rng.deal`, which is `AIHub.rng('pitches')` in\n250\t  a hub session. Cosmetic randomness (particles, crowd, babble variation) uses `BZ.rng.fx` or the engine's own. Rules,\n251\t  pitches, contact and AI never call `Math.random`.\n252\t- **Everything is an event.** Each outcome is emitted on the bus (`BZ.bus.emit('homer', { batter, dist, batzooka })`).\n253\t  Commentary, crowd, audio, HUD stamps, stats, meter gains, the hub reporter, the attract director and the tests all\n254\t  listen. A new listener never edits the rules. The events are listed in Appendix C.\n255\t- **Content is data.** Teams, players, parks, pitches, supers, hazards, lessons, story scenes, line pools, upgrades and\n256\t  songs are registry entries (`BZ.def('parks', 'caldera', {...})`), referenced by id. Adding a park or a team is a data\n257\t  entry, plus a hazard plug-in if it has one.\n258\t- **Live tuning.** Every balance number lives in `BZ.TUNE` and is described in `BZ.TUNE_META` (label, group, min, max,\n259\t  step, default; Appendix A). Code reads `TUNE.x` each time it needs it and never caches it, so dev sliders act at once.\n260\t- **Input through contexts.** Modules ask the context router (§6.14), never raw keys.\n261\t- **One debug handle.** `game.debug` serves the tests, the dev mode and the attract director (§11.2).\n262\t\n263\t### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n264\t\n265\t```\n266\tboot ──(AIHub.active and connect() gives info)──► hubDerby: card → countdown → round → end overlay\n267\t  └──► title ◄──────► attract   (after 10 s with no input on title or top menus; any input returns)\n268\t         ├─ TOUR ──► tourMenu (Continue · New · Load) ─► tourMap ⇄ clubhouse\n269\t         │             tourMap ─► story ─► match ─► results ─► story ─► tourMap … final ─► ending ─► credits ─► title\n270\t         ├─ EXHIBITION ─► teamSelect (join 1–4, sides, teams, park, innings, difficulty) ─► match ─► results\n271\t         ├─ DERBY ─► derbySetup (1–4 players, park, length) ─► derby ─► derbyResults (initials, table)\n272\t         ├─ SPRING TRAINING ─► lessonList ─► lesson ─► lessonList\n273\t         ├─ OPTIONS ─► game.menu('settings') (the engine's screens, with ours added)\n274\t         └─ ABOUT ─► about (About · Source Information · Feedback · Credits)\n275\t\n276\tpause menu, on every pausable scene: Resume · Playbook · Pitching Change* · Restart* · Dev Mode* · Settings · Controls ·\n277\tFull screen · Quit to title*      (* hidden in AI Hub sessions; Pitching Change only on defense in a match)\n278\t```\n279\t\n280\tDeep links for tests and checks: `#title`, `#attract`, `#match` (optionally `#match/<park>`), `#derby`, `#training/<n>`,\n281\t`#tour/<stop>`, `#about`, `#gallery` (the animation gallery), `#hubcard` (the hub card with a mock), `#touch`\n282\t(forces the touch-only title).\n283\t\n284\t`menu.items` is given as a function that returns entries, with falsy ones skipped. The engine's pause screen evaluates\n285\tit each time it opens (engine line ~9470: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`; confirm this in M0 for\n286\tthe standard menu).\n287\t\n288\t---\n289\t\n290\t## 5. The ballpark: units and geometry\n291\t\n292\t**Units.** 16 U (world units) is one tile. Players are `size: 1.25` rigs, about 33 U tall. The game shows distances in\n293\tfeet (`feet = U × 0.32`) and speeds in mph (`mph = U/s × 0.26`). Proportions are arcade, not real: the basepath shows as\n294\t96 ft and center field about 380 ft, which reads right.\n295\t\n296\t**Orientation.** Home plate H is in the south and center field in the north (−y); x runs east, so first base is on the\n297\tright of the screen. Spray angle θ is measured from straight-away center, + toward right field. Fair territory is\n298\t|θ| ≤ 45°.\n299\t\n300\t| Point | Offset from H (x, y) in U |\n301\t|---|---|\n302\t| plate front edge (the strike-zone plane) | (0, −6) |\n303\t| first base | (+212, −212) |\n304\t| second base | (0, −424) |\n305\t| third base | (−212, −212) |\n306\t| pitching rubber | (0, −205) |\n307\t| mound | circle r 26 at (0, −200), drawn raised 3 U (a shape; play stays at z 0) |\n308\t| batter's boxes | centers (−18, −4) for righties and (+18, −4) for lefties, 12 × 22 |\n309\t| catcher | (0, +16), crouched |\n310\t| umpire | (0, +30) |\n311\t| on-deck circles | (−150, +60) and (+150, +60) |\n312\t| dugouts | 3B side x −380..−250, y +20..+56; 1B side mirrored (cut-down walls with benches) |\n313\t\n314\t**Fielders' default spots:** P (0, −200) · C (0, +16) · 1B (+225, −275) · 2B (+105, −395) · SS (−105, −395) ·\n315\t3B (−225, −275) · LF (−420, −760) · CF (0, −900) · RF (+420, −760). A park can shift its outfielders back by its\n316\tdepth.\n317\t\n318\t**Fence.** `r = R(θ)`: a Catmull-Rom curve over θ through five control distances (LF line, LC gap, CF, RC gap, RF line;\n319\tper park in §7.2). The fence height `Hf(θ)` defaults to 40 U, with optional sections (the MegaDome's 90-U Mega Wall).\n320\tFoul poles stand at θ = ±45°, 150 U tall. **Foul walls** (the stands' front) run parallel to the foul lines, 70 U\n321\toutside them, from the backstop to the poles. The **backstop** is the line y = +96 from x −150 to +150, joined to the\n322\tfoul walls by 45° segments.\n323\t\n324\t**Surfaces.**\n325\t- Infield dirt: r ≤ 470 and |θ| ≤ 50°, minus the infield grass square (inside the basepaths, inset 22 U); plus 22-U\n326\t  basepath strips, the home circle (r 44) and the mound.\n327\t- Warning track: dirt from R(θ) − 48 to R(θ).\n328\t- Chalk: foul lines 2 U wide to the fence; batter's and catcher's boxes; coaches' boxes; on-deck circles.\n329\t- Grass: mowing pattern per park (stripes, checkerboard or diamonds), using `E.hash2` and `E.noise2` grain as the\n330\t  sports kit does.\n331\t- Beyond the fence: the park's own ground (cornfield, sea, lava, regolith, graves, neon concourse).\n332\t\n333\t**The TileMap is generated** by the park builder from these functions, one cell per 16 U (about 120 × 105 cells for a\n334\tpark with a 1,180-U center field). Floor tags: `grass`, `dirt`, `track`, `foul`, `out`, and per park `water`, `lava`,\n335\t`regolith`, `grave`, `concourse`. Wall types:\n336\t\n337\t| Type | Use | Settings |\n338\t|---|---|---|\n339\t| `F` | the fence | h = Hf, padded material per park, `roof: 'plain'` in the park's top-line color |\n340\t| `S1`–`S4` | stand tiers | h 52 / 64 / 76 / 88; face `'stone'`, `'plank'` or a park material |\n341\t| `B` | the backstop | h 64, net look |\n342\t| `D` | dugouts | h 20, `cut: true` |\n343\t\n344\tEverything south of the plate is `cut: true`, since it faces the camera. `floorTex` paints the lines and patterns. The\n345\tPlate Cam's Mode 7 floor is the same TileMap (`new E.Mode7({ floor: map, sky: park.backdrop })`).\n346\t\n347\t**Who collides with what.** The ball uses the analytic geometry (R(θ), wall segments, poles), not tiles. Players are\n348\t`E.Body`s that collide with the TileMap walls. The crowd sits on the stand tiles. Props (light towers, scoreboards,\n349\tpark features) are `r.shape` entries; solid ones are added with `map.solid`.\n350\t\n351\t---\n352\t\n353\t## 6. Systems\n354\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"0398fec2-4aae-4da9-b866-8def50b0caa3","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:35:13.808Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n### 4.1 Dev folder layout\n\n```\nBatzookaBaseball-Baseball-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174819/\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js          the engine: handoff v0.85.2 plus the changes in ENGINE-CHANGES.md\n  engine/mocap-readable.js     the repo's src/mocap/readable.js (unchanged)\n  engine/mocap.js              the repo's src/mocap/mocap.js (unchanged)\n  assets/mocap-batzooka.js     the clip set cut by tools/anim-set.mjs (§8.3)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js      AI Hub client 1.4.0, byte for byte\n  src/*.js                     the game: files concatenated by name into one IIFE (§4.2)\n  build/game.js                the concatenated game (written by tools/build.mjs; dev page and tests load it)\n  index.html                   dev page: engine, mocap, vendor client, build/game.js as separate scripts\n  tools/build.mjs              src → build/game.js; then the single-file bundle → ../<name>.html\n  tools/serve.mjs              a static server on 127.0.0.1, port 0 (prints the port; for the hub harness)\n  tools/check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-sheet.mjs, asset-find.mjs, asset-lib.mjs, asset-set.mjs (handoff)\n  tools/anim-set.mjs, mocap-lib.mjs (repo)\n  hub/harness.html, bridge.js, round.js, README.md (AI Hub)\n  tests/run.mjs                the one command (§11.1)\n  tests/lib/*.mjs              the headless loader, assertions, bots\n  tests/*.test.mjs             the tests (§11.3)\n  package.json                 { \"type\": \"module\", devDependencies: { \"playwright\": \"1.63.0\" }, scripts: { test, build, check } }\n  ENGINE-CHANGES.md            each engine change and why\n  NOTES.md                     tuning notes, decisions, the feedback answers as they come\n```\n\n### 4.2 Source files\n\n`tools/build.mjs` concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside one `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();`.\nEach file adds to one namespace object, `BZ`. Files never reach into each other's locals.\n\n| File | Holds |\n|---|---|\n| `00-core.js` | `BZ`; `BZ.def(kind, id, spec)` and `BZ.get(kind, id)` registries; the event bus `BZ.bus` (`on`, `off`, `emit`, a ring buffer of the last 200 events); RNG streams `BZ.rng.play / fx / deal`; `BZ.TUNE`, `BZ.TUNE_META`, `BZ.resetTune()`; unit helpers (`feet(u)`, `mph(uPerS)`, angle helpers); constants |\n| `01-input.js` | the action preset; input contexts and routing; device tracking; the glyph icon renderer and prompt builder; mouse helpers (pointer → strike-zone plane, pointer → ground → base); touch-only detection |\n| `02-audio.js` | SFX definitions; the crowd bed; the music director (song per scene, layers, stings, tempo); the **Babble** voice synth |\n| `03-rigs.js` | player rig factory (team uniform + player looks); custom poses and attack specs (swing, bunt, windup, throw, tag, dive, climb, umpire calls); the mocap driver (clip windows, mirrored clips for lefties, blending); Batzooka and mitt overdraw |\n| `04-shapes.js` | `E.SHAPES` entries for ballpark objects (§7.12) |\n| `05-park.js` | the park builder: geometry from a park def → TileMap rows, legend, types and `floorTex`; fence function, foul lines and bases; stand seats; light rigs; props; the Plate Cam's Mode 7 floor, sky and world billboards |\n| `10-rules.js` | the rules engine, pure logic: count, outs, bases, forces, scoring, half-innings, game end, extra innings, mercy rule; line score and box stats |\n| `11-pitch.js` | pitch catalog; selection; windup and release ring; flight paths; aftertouch; stamina; calls; the catcher's catch and passed balls |\n| `12-bat.js` | the sweet-spot reticle; swing types; the contact model; bunts; contact → launch |\n| `13-ball.js` | batted and thrown ball physics; prediction; fence, pole, stands and foul tests; home runs; ground-rule doubles; wind |\n| `14-field.js` | fielders: positions, roles, assignment, chase, catch, dive, jump, wall climb, throws, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns |\n| `15-run.js` | baserunners: the batter-runner, forces, tag-ups, advance and retreat orders, steals, leads, pickoffs, slides, bowl-overs, scoring |\n| `16-supers.js` | the Batzooka meter, the supers (pitch, bat, throw, run), turbo |\n| `17-rhubarb.js` | the bench-clearing brawl |\n| `18-hazards.js` | the six parks' hazards and features |\n| `20-ai.js` | CPU pitcher, batter, fielders and runners by difficulty; **the bot**, which turns a CPU brain's choices into player inputs (for tests, the playthrough and the attract mode) |\n| `21-cams.js` | Plate Cam, Broadcast Cam, Moonshot Cam, cut-ins, the replay ring buffer, transitions |\n| `22-hud.js` | scorebug, count, bases, meters, player cards, prompts, call stamps, radar, radio box, pitch and timing feedback |\n| `23-talk.js` | the commentary director (line pools, priorities, rate limit, variables), the PA and the umpire; the cutscene runner |\n| `24-crowd.js` | crowd seats and animation; hype; the wave; the jumbotron; the mascot; fireworks; between-innings shows |\n| `30-match.js` | the match scene: its phases, update order and draw order |\n| `31-derby.js` | the Derby scene (local hot-seat and hub) |\n| `32-training.js` | Spring Training lessons |\n| `33-tour.js` | Tour map, clubhouse, story flow, rewards, saves |\n| `34-menus.js` | title, mode select, team select and joining, results, Playbook (help), About/Credits, pause items |\n| `35-attract.js` | the attract director and its segments |\n| `36-dev.js` | the dev mode overlay |\n| `40-hub.js` | AI Hub session flow, its card, ghosts |\n| `50-teams.js` … `56-songs.js` | content data: teams, parks, story, lessons, lines, upgrades, songs |\n| `90-start.js` | boot steps, the scenes table, deep links, `game.debug`, `game.start` |\n\n### 4.3 The spine (rules every module follows)\n\n- **One match state, `BZ.M`** (fields in §6.1). The simulation runs only in `update(dt)` (exact 1/120 s steps); `draw`\n  only reads state. A whole match can run headless with `draw` skipped.\n- **Seeded gameplay.** Gameplay randomness comes only from `BZ.rng.play` (`E.rng(seed)`, re-seeded per match;\n  `game.debug.setup({ seed })`). The Derby's pitch sequence comes from `BZ.rng.deal`, which is `AIHub.rng('pitches')` in\n  a hub session. Cosmetic randomness (particles, crowd, babble variation) uses `BZ.rng.fx` or the engine's own. Rules,\n  pitches, contact and AI never call `Math.random`.\n- **Everything is an event.** Each outcome is emitted on the bus (`BZ.bus.emit('homer', { batter, dist, batzooka })`).\n  Commentary, crowd, audio, HUD stamps, stats, meter gains, the hub reporter, the attract director and the tests all\n  listen. A new listener never edits the rules. The events are listed in Appendix C.\n- **Content is data.** Teams, players, parks, pitches, supers, hazards, lessons, story scenes, line pools, upgrades and\n  songs are registry entries (`BZ.def('parks', 'caldera', {...})`), referenced by id. Adding a park or a team is a data\n  entry, plus a hazard plug-in if it has one.\n- **Live tuning.** Every balance number lives in `BZ.TUNE` and is described in `BZ.TUNE_META` (label, group, min, max,\n  step, default; Appendix A). Code reads `TUNE.x` each time it needs it and never caches it, so dev sliders act at once.\n- **Input through contexts.** Modules ask the context router (§6.14), never raw keys.\n- **One debug handle.** `game.debug` serves the tests, the dev mode and the attract director (§11.2).\n\n### 4.4 Scenes and flow\n\n```\nboot ──(AIHub.active and connect() gives info)──► hubDerby: card → countdown → round → end overlay\n  └──► title ◄──────► attract   (after 10 s with no input on title or top menus; any input returns)\n         ├─ TOUR ──► tourMenu (Continue · New · Load) ─► tourMap ⇄ clubhouse\n         │             tourMap ─► story ─► match ─► results ─► story ─► tourMap … final ─► ending ─► credits ─► title\n         ├─ EXHIBITION ─► teamSelect (join 1–4, sides, teams, park, innings, difficulty) ─► match ─► results\n         ├─ DERBY ─► derbySetup (1–4 players, park, length) ─► derby ─► derbyResults (initials, table)\n         ├─ SPRING TRAINING ─► lessonList ─► lesson ─► lessonList\n         ├─ OPTIONS ─► game.menu('settings') (the engine's screens, with ours added)\n         └─ ABOUT ─► about (About · Source Information · Feedback · Credits)\n\npause menu, on every pausable scene: Resume · Playbook · Pitching Change* · Restart* · Dev Mode* · Settings · Controls ·\nFull screen · Quit to title*      (* hidden in AI Hub sessions; Pitching Change only on defense in a match)\n```\n\nDeep links for tests and checks: `#title`, `#attract`, `#match` (optionally `#match/<park>`), `#derby`, `#training/<n>`,\n`#tour/<stop>`, `#about`, `#gallery` (the animation gallery), `#hubcard` (the hub card with a mock), `#touch`\n(forces the touch-only title).\n\n`menu.items` is given as a function that returns entries, with falsy ones skipped. The engine's pause screen evaluates\nit each time it opens (engine line ~9470: `v(this._game.o.menu.items)` then `.filter(Boolean)`; confirm this in M0 for\nthe standard menu).\n\n---\n\n## 5. The ballpark: units and geometry\n\n**Units.** 16 U (world units) is one tile. Players are `size: 1.25` rigs, about 33 U tall. The game shows distances in\nfeet (`feet = U × 0.32`) and speeds in mph (`mph = U/s × 0.26`). Proportions are arcade, not real: the basepath shows as\n96 ft and center field about 380 ft, which reads right.\n\n**Orientation.** Home plate H is in the south and center field in the north (−y); x runs east, so first base is on the\nright of the screen. Spray angle θ is measured from straight-away center, + toward right field. Fair territory is\n|θ| ≤ 45°.\n\n| Point | Offset from H (x, y) in U |\n|---|---|\n| plate front edge (the strike-zone plane) | (0, −6) |\n| first base | (+212, −212) |\n| second base | (0, −424) |\n| third base | (−212, −212) |\n| pitching rubber | (0, −205) |\n| mound | circle r 26 at (0, −200), drawn raised 3 U (a shape; play stays at z 0) |\n| batter's boxes | centers (−18, −4) for righties and (+18, −4) for lefties, 12 × 22 |\n| catcher | (0, +16), crouched |\n| umpire | (0, +30) |\n| on-deck circles | (−150, +60) and (+150, +60) |\n| dugouts | 3B side x −380..−250, y +20..+56; 1B side mirrored (cut-down walls with benches) |\n\n**Fielders' default spots:** P (0, −200) · C (0, +16) · 1B (+225, −275) · 2B (+105, −395) · SS (−105, −395) ·\n3B (−225, −275) · LF (−420, −760) · CF (0, −900) · RF (+420, −760). A park can shift its outfielders back by its\ndepth.\n\n**Fence.** `r = R(θ)`: a Catmull-Rom curve over θ through five control distances (LF line, LC gap, CF, RC gap, RF line;\nper park in §7.2). The fence height `Hf(θ)` defaults to 40 U, with optional sections (the MegaDome's 90-U Mega Wall).\nFoul poles stand at θ = ±45°, 150 U tall. **Foul walls** (the stands' front) run parallel to the foul lines, 70 U\noutside them, from the backstop to the poles. The **backstop** is the line y = +96 from x −150 to +150, joined to the\nfoul walls by 45° segments.\n\n**Surfaces.**\n- Infield dirt: r ≤ 470 and |θ| ≤ 50°, minus the infield grass square (inside the basepaths, inset 22 U); plus 22-U\n  basepath strips, the home circle (r 44) and the mound.\n- Warning track: dirt from R(θ) − 48 to R(θ).\n- Chalk: foul lines 2 U wide to the fence; batter's and catcher's boxes; coaches' boxes; on-deck circles.\n- Grass: mowing pattern per park (stripes, checkerboard or diamonds), using `E.hash2` and `E.noise2` grain as the\n  sports kit does.\n- Beyond the fence: the park's own ground (cornfield, sea, lava, regolith, graves, neon concourse).\n\n**The TileMap is generated** by the park builder from these functions, one cell per 16 U (about 120 × 105 cells for a\npark with a 1,180-U center field). Floor tags: `grass`, `dirt`, `track`, `foul`, `out`, and per park `water`, `lava`,\n`regolith`, `grave`, `concourse`. Wall types:\n\n| Type | Use | Settings |\n|---|---|---|\n| `F` | the fence | h = Hf, padded material per park, `roof: 'plain'` in the park's top-line color |\n| `S1`–`S4` | stand tiers | h 52 / 64 / 76 / 88; face `'stone'`, `'plank'` or a park material |\n| `B` | the backstop | h 64, net look |\n| `D` | dugouts | h 20, `cut: true` |\n\nEverything south of the plate is `cut: true`, since it faces the camera. `floorTex` paints the lines and patterns. The\nPlate Cam's Mode 7 floor is the same TileMap (`new E.Mode7({ floor: map, sky: park.backdrop })`).\n\n**Who collides with what.** The ball uses the analytic geometry (R(θ), wall segments, poles), not tiles. Players are\n`E.Body`s that collide with the TileMap walls. The crowd sits on the stand tiles. Props (light towers, scoreboards,\npark features) are `r.shape` entries; solid ones are added with `map.solid`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Systems\n","numLines":175,"startLine":180,"totalLines":2111}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01U3KJ4itkCSLQX1dsGiBT6c","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
