{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QnYQnRiwQwd41YSra3jArc","type":"tool_result","content":"356\t## 6. Systems\n357\t\n358\tEach system lists what it does, its numbers (starting values in `TUNE`, tuned in play) and its tests (IDs refer to §11.3).\n359\t\n360\t### 6.1 Match flow and the rules engine (`10-rules.js`, `30-match.js`)\n361\t\n362\t**State `BZ.M`:** `mode` ('tour' | 'exhibition' | 'training' | 'attract' | 'derby') · `park` · `teams[0]` = away,\n363\t`teams[1]` = home, each `{ def, lineup[9], order, pitcher, bullpen[], runs, hits, errors, meter, turbo, line[] }` ·\n364\t`inning`, `half` ('top' | 'bottom'), `outs`, `balls`, `strikes` · `bases[3]` (runner id or null) · `phase` · `ball` ·\n365\t`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n366\t`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\n367\town record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n368\t\n369\t**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n370\t(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\n371\tnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n372\t`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n373\t\n374\t**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\n375\ttwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\n376\twhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\n377\tare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\n378\tthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\n379\tfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\n380\tcatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\n381\thappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n382\t\n383\t**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\n384\tgame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, where the next run wins. The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\n385\tinnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n386\t\n387\t**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\n388\tappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\n389\twalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n390\t\n391\t**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\n392\tfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\n393\tpitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\n394\tchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n395\t\n396\t**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\n397\touts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\n398\tbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n399\t\n400\t### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n401\t\n402\t**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\n403\tslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\n404\tsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n405\t\n406\t**Delivery, for a human:**\n407\t1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button throws). The\n408\t   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n409\t2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n410\t   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n411\t3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n412\t   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n413\t   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n414\t   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n415\t4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n416\t   × the pitcher's Break stat × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n417\t\n418\t**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\n419\tits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n420\t\n421\t**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n422\t/ speed:\n423\t- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n424\t- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n425\t- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n426\t\n427\tSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n428\t(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's Velocity\n429\t× q × stamina × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n430\t\n431\t**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\n432\tThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\n433\tthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n434\t\n435\t**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n436\t`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\n437\tbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\n438\treliever.\n439\t\n440\t**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\n441\twild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's Glove/10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n442\t\n443\t**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\n444\tmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n445\t\n446\t**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\n447\tsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\n448\tedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n449\t\n450\t### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n451\t\n452\t**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n453\t±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\n454\tr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's Contact stat (0.85–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\n455\tthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n456\t\n457\t**Swings:**\n458\t\n459\t| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n460\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n461\t| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n462\t| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n463\t| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n464\t| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n465\t\n466\t**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n467\t|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\n468\td > 1.6 r. Otherwise:\n469\t- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n470\t  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n471\t- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n472\t- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n473\t  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n474\t  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n475\t- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n476\t  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n477\t- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × Power stat (0.85–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n478\t  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n479\t- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n480\t  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n481\t  hook to the pull side.\n482\t- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n483\t  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n484\t\n485\t**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\n486\tpitch crosses.\n487\t\n488\t**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\n489\tmirrored mocap swing.\n490\t\n491\t**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\n492\tgrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\n493\tBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\n494\tmapping).\n495\t\n496\t### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n497\t\n498\t**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n499\t- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n500\t- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n501\t- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n502\t- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n503\t\n504\t**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n505\t`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n506\t260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n507\t\n508\t**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n509\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n510\t- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n511\t- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n512\t\n513\t**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\n514\tsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n515\t\n516\t**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\n517\tthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\n518\tsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n519\t\n520\t**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\n521\tleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\n522\tland 30–60 U wide.\n523\t\n524\t**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n525\t(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\n526\tThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n527\t\n528\t**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\n529\troll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\n530\texactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n531\t\n532\t### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n533\t\n534\t**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\n535\tlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\n536\tfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\n537\tintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\n538\tto the next best.\n539\t\n540\t**Everyone else covers by role:**\n541\t- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n542\t- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n543\t- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n544\t- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n545\t- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"3506f501-ff0e-4abf-8d49-dc4ac75fb027","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:36:13.694Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 6. Systems\n\nEach system lists what it does, its numbers (starting values in `TUNE`, tuned in play) and its tests (IDs refer to §11.3).\n\n### 6.1 Match flow and the rules engine (`10-rules.js`, `30-match.js`)\n\n**State `BZ.M`:** `mode` ('tour' | 'exhibition' | 'training' | 'attract' | 'derby') · `park` · `teams[0]` = away,\n`teams[1]` = home, each `{ def, lineup[9], order, pitcher, bullpen[], runs, hits, errors, meter, turbo, line[] }` ·\n`inning`, `half` ('top' | 'bottom'), `outs`, `balls`, `strikes` · `bases[3]` (runner id or null) · `phase` · `ball` ·\n`fielders[9]` · `runners[]` · `batter`, `pitcher`, `catcher`, `umpire` · `control` (which human runs which job, §6.20) ·\n`stats` (per player: AB, H, 2B, 3B, HR, RBI, BB, K, SB; pitcher: IP, K, BB, pitches) · `seed` · `play` (the live play's\nown record: contact, who touched the ball, outs made, runs scored).\n\n**Phases:** `pregame` (lineup cards, \"PLAY BALL!\") → `halfStart` (\"TOP 1ST\" card; fielders jog out) → `atBat`\n(`walkUp` → `pitchSelect` → `windup` → `pitchFlight`) → `inPlay` (ball live) → `playOver` (calls, runs, replay) → the\nnext `atBat` or `halfEnd` (\"SIDE RETIRED\", line score, a between-innings show) → … → `final` (results). Side states:\n`cinematic`, `replay`, `rhubarb`, `between`.\n\n**Rules kept:** balls and strikes; four balls is a walk; three strikes is a strikeout; a foul is a strike except with\ntwo strikes, but a foul bunt with two strikes is a strikeout; hit by pitch sends the batter to first; fair and foul by\nwhere the ball lands or is first touched (past the bags) or where it stops or is touched (before them); caught flies\nare outs, fair or foul; tag-ups after a catch; force outs and tag outs; runs count when the runner touches home before\nthe third out, unless the third out is a force or the batter-runner is out before first; home runs (a fair fly over the\nfence, or off the pole); ground-rule doubles (a fair bounce over the fence); inside-the-park home runs; errors (a dropped\ncatch, a wild throw); stolen bases and pickoffs; wild pitches and passed balls; double plays (and triple plays when they\nhappen); walk-offs; the home team skips the bottom of the last inning when it leads.\n\n**Simplified:** extra innings start each half with a runner on second (the **Bonus Runner**). After 3 extra innings the\ngame goes to **SUDDEN BOOM**, where the next run wins. The **mercy rule** ends a game at a 10-run lead after 2 full\ninnings (a setting, on by default). Innings per game are 1, 3, 5 or 9 (Exhibition setting; Tour: 3, or 1 in Quick).\n\n**Dropped**, and listed on Spring Training's \"House Rules\" card as what you can't do: balks, the infield fly rule,\nappeals, interference and obstruction, check swings (every swing is committed), dropped third strikes, intentional\nwalks, substitutions other than a pitching change, and arguing with the umpire (\"believe us, we tried\").\n\n**Play over:** the play ends when (a) the ball is dead (home run, foul, caught third out, ground-rule double), or (b) a\nfielder holds the ball inside the infield dirt (r < 470) and no runner has moved toward a base for 0.6 s, or (c) the\npitcher holds it on the mound. A runner left between bases when (b) begins is played out: the AI fielder with the ball\nchases them (a simple rundown, §6.5) and they go back or on.\n\n**Tests:** T-RULES-01..24 (count, walks, strikeouts, foul with two strikes, foul bunt, HBP force chain, force and tag\nouts, the run-scoring timing on the third out, tag-ups, ground-rule double, home run with runners, walk-off, skipping the\nbottom of the last inning, extra innings with the Bonus Runner, SUDDEN BOOM, mercy rule, line score and box stats).\n\n### 6.2 Pitching (`11-pitch.js`)\n\n**Repertoire:** each pitcher has five pitches, one per button. Cross is the heater, Circle a breaking ball (curve or\nslider), Square an off-speed pitch (changeup, sinker or knuckler), Triangle their signature pitch, and R1 their team's\nsuper pitch (when the meter is full). Catalog in §7.3.\n\n**Delivery, for a human:**\n1. **Pick:** press a pitch button (keyboard K / L / J / I / E; mouse: the wheel picks, the left button throws). The\n   pitcher comes set and the windup starts.\n2. **Aim:** during the windup (0.9 s × the pitcher's tempo), the stick, WASD or mouse moves the **target marker** over\n   the zone plane, out to 8 U beyond its edges.\n3. **Release:** a **release ring** closes on the marker over the last 0.45 s of the windup. Press the same pitch button\n   (or the left mouse button) when it meets the marker's inner circle. Release quality q ∈ [0, 1]: perfect within ±40 ms\n   (× `TUNE.releaseWindow`), linear down to 0 at ±200 ms; no press means an auto-release with q = 0.3. q adds 0–8% speed\n   and sharpens the break by 0–20%. The miss radius around the target is lerp(5, 0.5, q) U × fatigue.\n4. **Aftertouch:** for the first 40% of the flight, the stick bends the ball up to ±4 U across and ±3 U up or down,\n   × the pitcher's Break stat × `TUNE.aftertouch`. It costs 0.5 stamina.\n\n**The CPU pitcher** (§6.11) picks from a count-and-batter table, aims at edges with an error by difficulty, rolls q from\nits difficulty, and uses aftertouch at higher levels.\n\n**Flight model.** Parametric and exact, so the plate crossing is known at release. With u = t / T, where T = distance\n/ speed:\n- `pos(u) = lerp(release, target + miss, u) + break(u) + after(u)`\n- `z(u)` adds a sag of `4 · u · (1 − u) · sag`, so the pitch arcs\n- `break(u) = [bx, bz] · u^k`, where k is the pitch's lateness exponent\n\nSpecial paths (zig-zag, vanish, wobble, wave) are functions of u (§7.3). The heater's base speed is 340 U/s\n(88 mph; flight about 0.57 s over the 193 U from release to the plate), × the pitch's factor × the pitcher's Velocity\n× q × stamina × `TUNE.pitchSpeed`.\n\n**Calls.** At the plate plane the ball's (x, z) is checked against the batter's zone, widened by the ball's radius (1.6 U).\nThe zone is 12 U wide (x −6..+6), from the knees (z 9 × size/1.25) to the chest (z 25 × size/1.25). A pitch in the zone\nthat isn't swung at is a strike, outside it a ball. The ball's crossing point shows as a dot for 1.5 s.\n\n**Stamina:** 100 at the start. A pitch costs heater 1.2, breaking 1.6, off-speed 1.0, signature 2.2, super 5 (×\n`TUNE.staminaDrain`). Below 40: speed ×0.92, miss ×1.4. Below 15 (\"gassed\"): ×0.85 and ×2. The CPU calls the bullpen\nbelow 20; a human sees a \"BULLPEN'S READY\" toast and can use Pitching Change in the pause menu. Each team has one\nreliever.\n\n**Catcher:** catches every pitch that crosses inside x ±16, z 0..34. A ball outside that, or in the dirt (z < 2), is a\nwild pitch or passed ball with chance 0.35 × (1 − catcher's Glove/10), and the ball rolls loose to the backstop.\n\n**Pickoffs:** before picking a pitch, L1 plus a base button throws over (the CPU does it rarely). A runner leading off\nmore than 20 U is tagged out if the throw beats them back.\n\n**Tests:** T-PITCH-01..12 (each pitch's crossing equals the planned target within the miss radius; flight time; break\nsize and lateness; aftertouch bounded; release quality → speed and miss; stamina drain and fatigue; calls at the zone\nedges; wild pitch chance; the super pitches' special paths; pickoff timing).\n\n### 6.3 Batting and the contact model (`12-bat.js`)\n\n**Reticle (the sweet spot).** A circle on the zone plane that the stick or WASD moves (80 U/s, eased) within the zone\n±6 U; the mouse places it directly (pointer → zone plane by the zone's projected corners, §6.14). Its radius is\nr = 4.2 U for a contact swing and 2.8 U for a power swing, × the batter's Contact stat (0.85–1.15) × `TUNE.sweetSpot` ×\nthe difficulty's assist. It is drawn as a ring with a dot at its center, and a faint outer ring at 1.6 r (foul-tip range).\n\n**Swings:**\n\n| Button | Swing | Delay to the plate plane | Radius | Max exit speed |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ✕ / K / Space / left click | Contact | 0.10 s | 4.2 | 720 U/s |\n| □ / J / right click | Power | 0.14 s | 2.8 | 860 U/s |\n| R1 / E, then a swing button | Batzooka (meter full; arms the next swing) | 0.16 s | 6.3 | 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6) ≈ 1,250 U/s, plus a rocket boost |\n| △ / I, held | Bunt (square around; let go to pull back) | none: contact when the ball crosses inside the bunt radius 6 | 6 | 160 U/s |\n\n**Contact.** For a swing pressed at ts, the timing error is `e = ts + delay − tCross` (+ is late). The swing misses if\n|e| > the OK window (90 ms × `TUNE.swingTiming` × assist) or if the distance from the ball to the reticle center is\nd > 1.6 r. Otherwise:\n- `dn = d / r`. When 1 < dn ≤ 1.6 the swing is a **foul tip**: the ball goes back or out of play (foul), or a weak\n  dribbler (0.3 chance).\n- `timing = 1 − (|e| / window)^1.5`, `Q = (1 − min(dn, 1)) · timing` (0..1).\n- **Spray:** `θ = (e / window) · 48° + ((bx − rx) / r) · 10° + noise(±4°)` for a right-handed batter: early (e < 0)\n  pulls to left field (θ < 0), late goes the other way, and an outside pitch (bx > rx) drifts toward right field.\n  Negate the timing term for lefties (early pulls to right field). |θ| > 45° is foul.\n- **Launch:** `φ = 12° − ((rz − bz) / r) · 35°`. Swinging above the ball (the reticle higher than the ball) drives a\n  grounder; swinging under it lofts a fly. A power swing adds +6°; a Batzooka swing clamps φ to 28°–38°.\n- **Exit speed:** `EV = lerp(220, EVmax, Q^1.3) × Power stat (0.85–1.15) × (0.9 + 0.1 · pitchSpeed / 340) ×\n  TUNE.exitVelocity × park.evMul`.\n- **Velocity:** `vx = EV cos φ sin θ`, `vy = −EV cos φ cos θ`, `vz = EV sin φ`. Sidespin has magnitude 3 · |e| (e in\n  seconds) and curves the ball further toward the side it was hit: late swings slice to the opposite field, early ones\n  hook to the pull side.\n- **Feedback** (on for Rookie, Training and the \"Timing feedback\" setting): \"EARLY 34 ms\", \"PERFECT!\", \"LATE 12 ms\",\n  \"UNDER IT\", \"TOPPED\".\n\n**Whiffs:** a swing and miss is a strike. A swing with no ball in reach (too early) still counts as a strike when the\npitch crosses.\n\n**Batter's box:** righties stand in the box on the third-base side and lefties on the first-base side. Lefties use the\nmirrored mocap swing.\n\n**Tests:** T-BAT-01..14 (perfect center → line drive up the middle; early → pull, late → opposite field; reticle high →\ngrounder, low → fly; outside 1.6 r → miss, between r and 1.6 r → foul tip; power vs contact radius and exit speed;\nBatzooka forced loft; bunt exit; foul beyond 45°; lefty mirror; the assist multipliers; the reticle clamp; mouse\nmapping).\n\n### 6.4 Ball physics (`13-ball.js`)\n\n**Integration** at 120 Hz: `v += (g + drag + spin + wind) · dt`, `p += v · dt`.\n- g = (0, 0, −520 × park.gravityMul × `TUNE.gravity`)\n- drag = −0.05 · v (`TUNE.drag`)\n- spin = sidespin × speed × 0.004, across the path\n- wind = (wx, wy) × windEffect(z), where windEffect is 0 below 60 U and 1 above 160\n\n**Ground:** at z ≤ 0 the ball bounces: `vz = −vz · e` (grass 0.42, dirt 0.50, track 0.45, regolith 0.55; ×\n`TUNE.bounce`), and the speed along the ground ×0.82 on grass and ×0.90 on dirt. Once |vz| < 25 it rolls, slowing by\n260 U/s² on grass and 180 on dirt (× `TUNE.rollFriction`) until it stops.\n\n**Fence:** a fair ball with r > R(θ) − 1.6:\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) and the ball hasn't touched the ground → **HOME RUN**;\n- z ≥ Hf(θ) after a bounce → **GROUND-RULE DOUBLE**;\n- otherwise it bounces off the fence: the radial velocity reverses × 0.35 and the tangential part × 0.8.\n\n**Poles:** a ball within 3 U of a pole below 150 U is a home run (\"off the foul pole!\"). **Foul walls and backstop:**\nsegment collisions with reflection × 0.4. A ball over them is out of play (foul), drawn flying into the crowd.\n\n**Prediction** (`BZ.ball.predict(ball, secs)`): forward simulation with the same integrator and no randomness. It gives\nthe first landing point and time, the moments when the ball comes down through catch heights (z ≤ 34 descending), and\nsamples along the path every 1/30 s for grounder intercepts. Fielding AI, the landing marker and the cameras use it.\n\n**Thrown balls:** `E.arc(from, to, g, { time })`, with the flight time from distance / throw speed and an apex of at\nleast 6 U. A throw is caught when it passes within the receiver's catch radius at z 0..40. Off-target (error) throws\nland 30–60 U wide.\n\n**Display height:** in the Broadcast Cam the ball and its trail are drawn at `z' = z < 40 ? z : 40 + (z − 40) · 0.45`\n(`TUNE.heightSquash`), so high flies stay near their shadows on screen. Catching happens at glove heights, where z' = z.\nThe true shadow and the landing marker (a pulsing X with a ring) are always drawn.\n\n**Tests:** T-BALL-01..12 (range of a 30° launch vs the closed form within 5% before drag; drag lowers range; bounce and\nroll stop; fence bounce vs home run vs ground-rule double; poles; foul walls; prediction equals the simulation\nexactly; wind; moon gravity; the display squash keeps z' = z at glove height).\n\n### 6.5 Fielding (`14-field.js`)\n\n**Assignment at contact.** For each fielder, compute the earliest **intercept**: for a fly, reaching the predicted\nlanding point (or a catch-height crossing) before the ball; for a grounder, the first sampled path point where the\nfielder's time to get there (reaction + distance / speed) is no more than the ball's. The fielder with the smallest\nintercept time is the **chaser**. If the defense is human, the chaser is the controlled fielder, and L1 / Q switches\nto the next best.\n\n**Everyone else covers by role:**\n- 1B covers first; if 1B is the chaser, P covers it.\n- 2B covers second on balls to the left side, SS on balls to the right side.\n- 3B covers third; C covers home.\n- P backs up home or third on hits to the outfield.\n- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.","numLines":190,"startLine":356,"totalLines":2114}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01QnYQnRiwQwd41YSra3jArc","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
