{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2kCsSraPvpKvWjK9sv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D9o5kZbH8feLUbgJwyNdZm","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":546,"limit":225},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1712,"cache_read_input_tokens":447357,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1712},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"ef1e6e00-aada-4571-a66a-3a9de307b0f9","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:36:35.447Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2kCe43GZTTDgC5wFEY","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01D9o5kZbH8feLUbgJwyNdZm":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":546,"limit":225}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D9o5kZbH8feLUbgJwyNdZm","type":"tool_result","content":"546\t- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n547\t- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n548\t  the outfielder.\n549\t\n550\t**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × Speed stat × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\n551\ttime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n552\t\n553\t**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × Glove stat × `TUNE.catchRadius`\n554\tacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\n555\tin a wall climb. Success:\n556\t`p = clamp(0.985 × glove(0.9..1.05) − speedPenalty (−0.05..−0.15 above 600 U/s) − 0.08 if diving − difficulty, 0.5, 0.995)`.\n557\tA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\n558\tdizzy-stars gag.\n559\t\n560\t**Throwing:**\n561\t- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard L, I, J, K; mouse: click the base in the\n562\t  world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × Arm stat\n563\t  (0.85–1.2) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n564\t- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n565\t- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n566\t  presses the base again.\n567\t- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n568\t  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n569\t  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n570\t  play).\n571\t\n572\t**Outs on the bases:**\n573\t- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n574\t- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n575\t  swipe animation.\n576\t- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n577\t  they throw when the runner turns.\n578\t\n579\t**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n580\t**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n581\t18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n582\t\n583\t**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\n584\titself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n585\t\n586\t**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\n587\trange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\n588\ttries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\n589\tbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n590\t\n591\t### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n592\t\n593\t**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × Speed stat (0.85–1.2) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\n594\trunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n595\t`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n596\t- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n597\t- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n598\t- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n599\t  (tag up) before advancing.\n600\t\n601\t**Orders (human):**\n602\t- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n603\t- L1 / Q: send all back.\n604\t- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n605\t  keyboard L, I, J, K).\n606\t- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n607\t\n608\t**Steals and leads:**\n609\t- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n610\t- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n611\t- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n612\t- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n613\t\n614\t**Slides:**\n615\t- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n616\t  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n617\t- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n618\t\n619\t**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls Power + 1d6\n620\t(`rng.play`) against the catcher's Power + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\n621\tcatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n622\t\n623\t**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n624\t\n625\t**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n626\t\n627\t**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\n628\ttag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\n629\tan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\n630\tpickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n631\t\n632\t### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n633\t\n634\t**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\n635\tfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n636\t\n637\t| Event | Gain |\n638\t|---|---|\n639\t| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n640\t| single | +10 |\n641\t| extra-base hit | +15 |\n642\t| home run | +25 |\n643\t| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n644\t| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n645\t| double play | +15 |\n646\t| stolen base | +10 |\n647\t| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n648\t| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n649\t| a park target hit | +20 |\n650\t\n651\tAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n652\t\n653\t**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n654\t\n655\t| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n656\t|---|---|---|---|\n657\t| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n658\t| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n659\t| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n660\t| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n661\t\n662\tThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\n663\tinnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n664\t\n665\t**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\n666\tfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\n667\tmeter clamps at 100).\n668\t\n669\t### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n670\t\n671\t**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n672\t10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\n673\tThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\n674\tanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n675\t\n676\t### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n677\t\n678\t**Triggers:**\n679\t- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n680\t- a bowl-over (0.25);\n681\t- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n682\t- the dev trigger.\n683\t\n684\tSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n685\t\n686\t**Flow:**\n687\t1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n688\t2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n689\t   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n690\t3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n691\t   defense); the rest are AI.\n692\t4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n693\t5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n694\t   is really ejected.\n695\t6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n696\t\n697\t**Fighting:**\n698\t- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n699\t  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n700\t- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n701\t- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n702\t- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n703\t  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n704\t\n705\t**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n706\t\n707\t**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\n708\ton first after; no stray state after it ends).\n709\t\n710\t### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n711\t\n712\tEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\n713\trate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\n714\tline the first time it happens.\n715\t\n716\t| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n717\t|---|---|---|\n718\t| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n719\t| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n720\t| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n721\t| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n722\t| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n723\t| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n724\t\n725\t**Wind** (every park but the dome on the moon): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\n726\tballs above 60 U. A flag and windsock show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n727\t\n728\t**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\n729\tthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n730\t\n731\t### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n732\t\n733\tFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n734\t\n735\t| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n736\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n737\t| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n738\t| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n739\t| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n740\t| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n741\t| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n742\t| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n743\t| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n744\t| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n745\t\n746\tThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n747\t\n748\t- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n749\t  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n750\t  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n751\t  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n752\t- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n753\t  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n754\t- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n755\t  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n756\t- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n757\t  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n758\t  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n759\t\n760\t**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over 30 seeded 9-inning games at Pro: runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\n761\tplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\n762\tover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\n763\tones).\n764\t\n765\t### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n766\t\n767\t**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n768\t`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n769\t- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n770\t- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"73a52676-b7d0-47cc-99c4-2656946b73ea","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:36:35.453Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"- The outfielder nearest the play backs it up.\n- On a ball to the deep outfield, the SS (left side) or 2B (right side) goes out as the **cutoff man**, halfway to\n  the outfielder.\n\n**Moving:** fielders are `E.Body` at 132 U/s × Speed stat × `TUNE.fielderSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35 (§6.8). CPU reaction\ntime comes from the difficulty (§6.11); a human's control starts at once.\n\n**Catching:** a catch is attempted when the ball is within the catch radius (9 U × Glove stat × `TUNE.catchRadius`\nacross the ground) and the ball's z is in the glove range: 0..34 standing, up to 70 at a jump's top, or up to Hf + 30\nin a wall climb. Success:\n`p = clamp(0.985 × glove(0.9..1.05) − speedPenalty (−0.05..−0.15 above 600 U/s) − 0.08 if diving − difficulty, 0.5, 0.995)`.\nA failed catch is an **error**: the ball pops loose (vz 80, random direction from `rng.play`), with a \"clank\" and a\ndizzy-stars gag.\n\n**Throwing:**\n- A human presses a base button: ○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home (keyboard L, I, J, K; mouse: click the base in the\n  world, §6.14). Hold to power up: a 0.6 s meter on the fielder; let go to throw. Throw speed is 560 U/s × Arm stat\n  (0.85–1.2) × (0.85 + 0.35 · power) × `TUNE.throwSpeed`.\n- Above 85% power the throw can go wild: `p = (power − 0.85) × 0.8`, plus fatigue.\n- With a cutoff man up, a throw to a base far away goes through him; he relays automatically (CPU), or the human\n  presses the base again.\n- **CPU throws** (and the \"Auto throw\" assist): for each runner in danger, lead runner first, compare the runner's time\n  to the next base with the throw's time. Throw where the out is likely and worth the most; with no likely out, throw to\n  the base ahead of the lead runner. After a force at second, the pivot man goes on to first by himself (the double\n  play).\n\n**Outs on the bases:**\n- **Force out:** a fielder holding the ball touches the forced base (within 8 U) before the runner arrives.\n- **Tag out:** a fielder holding the ball comes within 10 U of a runner who is off base. The tag is automatic, with a\n  swipe animation.\n- **Rundown:** the fielder with the ball runs at the runner; a second fielder covers the base the runner heads for;\n  they throw when the runner turns.\n\n**Dive** (R2 / C, or Ctrl): lunges 22 U along the facing in 0.35 s, low (z 0..14), and stays prone for 0.6 s.\n**Jump** (R1 / E without the ball): a jump (vz 260, × park.jumpMul), with the glove up to 70. **Wall climb**: R1 within\n18 U of the fence while the ball is coming high climbs the fence face (z up to Hf + 26) to **rob a home run**.\n\n**Assists (setting \"Fielding\")**: *Manual* (you run and throw); *Assisted* (the default: the chaser runs to the ball by\nitself, you throw); *Auto* (the CPU fields and throws, and you can still take over by moving the stick).\n\n**Tests:** T-FIELD-01..16 (the chaser on routine flies and grounders; cover roles by situation; catch radius and glove\nrange; jump and wall-climb ranges; a robbed home run counts as an out; dive; error rates in bounds over 1,000 seeded\ntries; a throw's time; the force at first beats a slow runner on a routine grounder; double play 6-4-3; tag out off the\nbase; rundown resolves; cutoff relay; assisted mode reaches the ball; mouse click-to-throw picks the base).\n\n### 6.6 Baserunning (`15-run.js`)\n\n**Runners** move along the basepath segments at 128 U/s × Speed stat (0.85–1.2) × `TUNE.runSpeed`, with turbo ×1.35. A\nrunner has a base (0 = the batter), a target, a progress, a state (`onBase`, `lead`, `running`, `returning`, `safe`,\n`out`, `scored`) and the flags `forced` and `mustTagUp`.\n- The **batter-runner** drops the bat and runs to first 0.25 s after contact, and may overrun first safely.\n- **Forced** runners must advance. **Unforced** runners follow the AI, or the human's orders.\n- On a **fly ball**, runners go halfway (AI) until it is caught or drops. After a catch they must go back and touch\n  (tag up) before advancing.\n\n**Orders (human):**\n- R1 / E: advance all runners one base.\n- L1 / Q: send all back.\n- Hold a base button plus R1 or L1: only the runner heading to that base (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home;\n  keyboard L, I, J, K).\n- Mouse: left-click a base to send the runner who can reach it; right-click a base to send that runner back.\n\n**Steals and leads:**\n- Before the pitch, R1 / E takes an extra lead (+12 U; easier pickoffs).\n- Hold L1 / Q and press the target base's button to **steal**: the runner breaks on the windup.\n- The catcher throws (AI) to the base, and it's a tag race.\n- The CPU runners steal by speed vs the catcher's arm and the pitch type.\n\n**Slides:**\n- Within 28 U of a base on a close play, runners slide feet-first automatically (mocap `Slide_Start`, `Slide_Loop`,\n  `Slide_Exit`), with dust.\n- ✕ / K in the last 40 U is a **headfirst dive**: +10% speed over the last 20 U, more dust, and a hype bonus.\n\n**Bowl-over:** a runner on turbo who reaches home while the catcher holds the ball for a tag rolls Power + 1d6\n(`rng.play`) against the catcher's Power + 1d6 + 1. If the runner wins, the ball pops loose and he's safe; if the\ncatcher wins, he's out and bounces off. Either way it's \"COLLISION!\": a dust burst, stars, crowd \"OOOH\".\n\n**Scoring:** a runner who touches home (within 6 U) scores, subject to the third-out rule (§6.1).\n\n**Assists (setting \"Baserunning\")**: *Manual* or *Auto* (the CPU runs your runners; you can still give orders).\n\n**Tests:** T-RUN-01..14 (forced advance on a walk with the bases loaded; the batter-runner overruns first safely;\ntag-up on a deep fly scores from third; leaving early on a caught fly means doubled off; advance all and retreat all;\nan individual order; a steal succeeds with a fast runner and is thrown out with a slow one; extra lead raises the\npickoff risk; the slide triggers; the headfirst bonus; bowl-over both ways; turbo speed; the mouse orders).\n\n### 6.7 The Batzooka meter and supers (`16-supers.js`)\n\n**The meter** (per team, 0..100) is the crowd's roar. The jumbotron, the crowd's animation and a music layer all\nfollow it. Gains, × `TUNE.meterGain` × the Comeback Boost (×1.5 for a team trailing by 3 or more, a setting):\n\n| Event | Gain |\n|---|---|\n| contact | +6 to +15 by Q |\n| single | +10 |\n| extra-base hit | +15 |\n| home run | +25 |\n| strikeout (to the pitcher's team) | +12 |\n| diving catch or wall catch | +20 |\n| double play | +15 |\n| stolen base | +10 |\n| headfirst slide safe | +5 |\n| winning a Rhubarb | +50 |\n| a park target hit | +20 |\n\nAt 100: \"BATZOOKA READY!\", a jumbotron flash, the crowd on its feet, and the music's \"hype\" layer comes in.\n\n**Supers** (what they cost from the meter):\n\n| Super | Who | Cost | Effect |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **BATZOOKA!** | batter: R1 / E arms it, then any swing | 100 | The bat transforms (glowing barrel, nozzle and fins drawn over the bat); the reticle is 1.5× larger and on fire; on contact EVmax = 780 × `TUNE.batzookaPower` (1.6, about 1,250 U/s), loft 28–38°, a rocket boost along v of 900 U/s² for 0.5 s, a smoke and fire trail, the Moonshot Cam. Miss and it's \"FIZZLE!\": a comic smoke puff, and the meter is spent. |\n| **Team super pitch** | pitcher: R1 / E at pitch select | 100 | The team's super path and effect (§7.3); 1.35× speed |\n| **Laser Arm** | fielder holding the ball: R1 plus a base button | 50 | A flat, nearly instant throw (1,400 U/s) with a laser trail, never wild |\n| **Afterburners** | runners: R1 plus L2 while running | 50 | All runners at ×1.8 for 3 s, with fire trails behind their feet |\n\nThe CPU uses supers when its meter is full and the moment is right: a Batzooka with runners on, or behind in the late\ninnings; a super pitch with two strikes.\n\n**Tests:** T-SUPER-01..10 (gains per event; the comeback multiplier; arming and spending; the Batzooka loft and boost;\nfizzle; each super pitch's path; Laser Arm speed and accuracy; Afterburners duration; the CPU's use conditions; the\nmeter clamps at 100).\n\n### 6.8 Turbo and athletic moves\n\n**Turbo** (per team, 0..100) is held on L2 / Shift by runners or the controlled fielder. It drains 40/s and refills\n10/s when not in use, plus 15 per pitch (× `TUNE.turboDrain`, `TUNE.turboPower`). The CPU uses it on close plays.\nThe dive, jump, wall climb, slides and bowl-over are specified in §6.5 and §6.6. Every athletic move has its own\nanimation (§8.3), a sound and a particle beat.\n\n### 6.9 The Rhubarb: the bench-clearing brawl (`17-rhubarb.js`)\n\n**Triggers:**\n- a hit-by-pitch at 0.9 × the heater's base speed or faster, with chance `TUNE.rhubarbChance` (0.4);\n- a bowl-over (0.25);\n- the Tour's scripted moment at Caldera Field;\n- the dev trigger.\n\nSetting \"Rhubarbs: Off\" turns them off; an HBP then just awards the base.\n\n**Flow:**\n1. \"RHUBARB!\" stamp; the Broadcast Cam frames the plate area.\n2. Six per side pour out of the dugouts (the batter, the on-deck hitter and the nearest teammates against the pitcher,\n   the catcher and nearby fielders).\n3. 12 seconds of brawling on the dirt around home. Each human controls one fighter (the batter, or the pitcher for the\n   defense); the rest are AI.\n4. The umpire's whistle: \"BREAK IT UP!\" Everyone dusts off and walks back.\n5. The side with more knockdowns gets +50 meter; a tie gives both +20. A comic \"ejection\" line follows, though nobody\n   is really ejected.\n6. The batter takes first base for the HBP.\n\n**Fighting:**\n- Stick: move. ✕ / K: punch combo (`E.Combo(['jab', 'cross', 'hook'])`). □ / J: shove (`'bash'`, `E.knockback` 160).\n  △ / I: grab and toss (within 12 U: the `throw` move; the foe flies 40 U). ○ / L: dodge (a dash).\n- Three hits knock a fighter down: pose `down`, dizzy stars for 2 s, then up again.\n- AI fighters approach the nearest foe and throw combos with a 0.25 s wind-up so they telegraph.\n- Fights between AI fighters with no human nearby are drawn as **fight clouds**: a smoke ball (`particles.smoke`) with\n  fists, feet and a cap popping out.\n\n**Tone:** cartoon slapstick only: bonks, slide whistles, \"POW\" stamps, no blood.\n\n**Tests:** T-RHU-01..06 (trigger conditions and the setting; duration; scoring by knockdowns; meter award; the batter\non first after; no stray state after it ends).\n\n### 6.10 Hazards, park features and wind (`18-hazards.js`)\n\nEach park has one hazard or feature, as a plug-in `{ init(M), update(M, dt), onBall(M, ball), draw(r), hud() }`. Its\nrate is × `TUNE.hazardRate`. It is always **telegraphed** (a sound, a warning stamp, smoke) and introduced by a radio\nline the first time it happens.\n\n| Park | Hazard / feature | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kernel Field | **Silo Shot** | A home run that hits the silo beyond right field pops it like popcorn: popcorn rains, +300 Fan Bucks, +20 meter. Corn stalks sway with the wind. |\n| Barnacle Bay Park | **Gull Patrol** and **Splash Zone** | Gulls circle above 200 U. A fly ball with its apex above 200 has a 25% chance per gull pass of being snatched; the gull carries it 60–140 U, then drops it from z 150 (still in play). A home run to right field lands in the bay: a splash and a boat horn. |\n| Caldera Field | **Lava Vents** | Two vents in the gaps (r 820, θ ±22°) erupt every 25 s (3 s of rumble and smoke first). A ball touching an erupting vent is launched (vz +600, a random push from `rng.play`); CPU fielders keep 30 U away and a human is knocked back. Ash falls; the outfield glows. |\n| Tranquility Dome | **Low Gravity** and **Meteor Showers** | Gravity ×0.55, exit speed ×0.8, jumps ×2, deep fences. Every 40 s a meteor shower leaves 3 craters (decals) in the outfield for the rest of the half-inning; a grounder rolling through one turns by ±40°. |\n| Boneyard Ballpark | **Phantom Fog** and **Ghost Gloves** | Fly balls above 120 U go invisible (only the shadow and the landing marker show) until they come back down. The Haunts' fielders reach +40 U in wall catches (they phase into the fence). |\n| MegaMax MegaDome | **Defense Drones**, the **MEGA-TRON** and the **Boss** | Three drones patrol the outfield sky (z 150–250) on `E.Path`s. A fly ball that hits one deflects; a Batzooka hit destroys it (an explosion, +150 Fan Bucks). A ball off the MEGA-TRON jumbotron in center field is worth +300. The boss rules are in §7.1. |\n\n**Wind** (every park but the dome on the moon): set per game from `rng.play`: a direction and 0–12 U/s² of push on\nballs above 60 U. A flag and windsock show it, and a HUD arrow with mph. On the moon the radio jokes that there's no air.\n\n**Tests:** T-HAZ-01..08 (each hazard fires at its rate and has its effect on a scripted ball; telegraphs come before\nthe event; the hazard rate slider; wind changes range in the right direction).\n\n### 6.11 CPU AI and difficulty (`20-ai.js`)\n\nFour levels: **ROOKIE, PRO, ALL-STAR, BATZOOKA**.\n\n| | Rookie | Pro | All-Star | Batzooka |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| CPU swing timing σ | 60 ms | 42 ms | 30 ms | 20 ms |\n| CPU aim error σ | 3.6 U | 2.6 U | 1.8 U | 1.2 U |\n| CPU discipline (takes a ball outside the zone) | 0.45 | 0.60 | 0.72 | 0.82 |\n| CPU release quality (mean) | 0.50 | 0.68 | 0.80 | 0.90 |\n| CPU aftertouch use | 0 | 0.3 | 0.6 | 0.9 |\n| CPU fielder reaction | 0.34 s | 0.25 s | 0.19 s | 0.14 s |\n| CPU pitch speed | ×0.85 | ×1.0 | ×1.07 | ×1.15 |\n| Human sweet spot and timing window | ×1.25 | ×1.0 | ×1.0 | ×0.9 |\n\nThe CPU skills are also × `TUNE.cpuBat`, `cpuPitch`, `cpuField` and `cpuRun`.\n\n- **CPU batter:** after its reaction delay it predicts the crossing point with an aim error and moves the reticle\n  there. It decides to swing from P(in zone) and its discipline, with a timing error from N(0, σ). It uses power swings\n  in hitter's counts (2-0, 3-1) and when it is strong; bunts now and then with a runner on first and no outs; and a\n  Batzooka when its meter is full.\n- **CPU pitcher:** a pitch-mix table by count (ahead: break it off; behind: heater) with the batter's power in mind. It\n  targets the corners, more as the level rises, and uses its super with two strikes when its meter is full.\n- **CPU fielders:** §6.5. **CPU runners:** advance by the time race with a margin from the level, tag up when they can\n  make it, steal by speed against the arm.\n- **The bot** (`BZ.bot`): runs these brains for player 1's side and turns their choices into **input actions**\n  (`input.hold`, `input.tap`, the reticle's target as stick moves). Tests, the scripted playthrough and the attract\n  mode use it to play the real game through the real controls.\n\n**Tests:** T-AI-01..08 (CPU vs CPU over 30 seeded 9-inning games at Pro: runs per team per game between 3 and 9; K% of\nplate appearances 12–32%; HR per plate appearance 2–8%; batting average .230–.330; errors per game 0.2–1.5; no game\nover 40 minutes of game time; no stuck play, meaning every play ends within 20 s; harder levels win more against easier\nones).\n\n### 6.12 Cameras, transitions and replays (`21-cams.js`)\n\n**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.","numLines":225,"startLine":546,"totalLines":2115}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D9o5kZbH8feLUbgJwyNdZm","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
