{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1xPEMPuU1gpqq6syeeepP","type":"tool_result","content":"768\t\n769\t**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n770\t`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n771\t- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n772\t- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n773\t- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n774\t  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n775\t  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n776\t  a super).\n777\t- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n778\t- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n779\t- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n780\t  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n781\t- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n782\t\n783\t**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n784\t(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n785\t`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n786\t- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n787\t- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n788\t\n789\t**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n790\t- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n791\t- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n792\t- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n793\t\n794\tZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\n795\tthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n796\t\n797\t**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n798\t1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n799\t2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n800\t   point);\n801\t3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n802\t   skippable with ✕).\n803\t\n804\tThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n805\t\n806\t**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\n807\trig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\n808\tthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\n809\tcorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n810\t\n811\t**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n812\t\n813\t**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\n814\tthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\n815\tscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\n816\trestored).\n817\t\n818\t### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n819\t\n820\t**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n821\t`game.cornerArea()`:\n822\t- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n823\t  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n824\t- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n825\t  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n826\t- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n827\t  pitcher's stamina bar.\n828\t- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n829\t  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n830\t- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n831\t  advance).\n832\t- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n833\t  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n834\t- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n835\t- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n836\t  the ball.\n837\t- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n838\t  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n839\t- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n840\t  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n841\t\n842\t**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\n843\tTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\n844\ttoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n845\t\n846\t**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\n847\treturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n848\t- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n849\t  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n850\t- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n851\t- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n852\t- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n853\t- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n854\t- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n855\t- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n856\t\n857\t**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n858\t- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n859\t  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n860\t- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n861\t\n862\t**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\n863\tfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\n864\tthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n865\t\n866\t### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n867\t\n868\t**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\n869\tbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\n870\tThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. The keyboard's I J K L cluster sits like the face buttons.\n871\t\n872\t| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n873\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n874\t| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | K, Space | left button |\n875\t| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n876\t| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n877\t| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n878\t| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q | – |\n879\t| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n880\t| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n881\t| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n882\t| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n883\t| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n884\t| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n885\t| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n886\t| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n887\t| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n888\t| pitch type | face buttons | | K L J I | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n889\t\n890\t**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n891\t\n892\t| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n893\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n894\t| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n895\t| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n896\t| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n897\t| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n898\t| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n899\t| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n900\t| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n901\t\n902\t- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n903\t  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n904\t  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n905\t- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n906\t  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n907\t  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n908\t  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n909\t- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n910\t  use.\n911\t- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n912\t- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n913\t  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n914\t- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n915\t  setting).\n916\t\n917\t**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n918\t(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel picks pitch\n919\ttypes; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\n920\tof its contexts' prompts).\n921\t\n922\t### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n923\t\n924\t**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\n925\tslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\n926\tgusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n927\t\n928\t**The commentary director:**\n929\t- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n930\t- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n931\t  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n932\t- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n933\t- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n934\t- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n935\t- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n936\t  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n937\t- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n938\t\n939\t**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n940\t- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n941\t- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n942\t  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n943\t- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n944\t- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n945\t- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n946\t\n947\t**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n948\t- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n949\t  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n950\t- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n951\t  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n952\t    offset from a hash.\n953\t  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n954\t  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n955\t    10-ms noise burst.\n956\t- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n957\t  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n958\t- **Voice profiles:**\n959\t\n960\t| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n961\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n962\t| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n963\t| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n964\t| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n965\t| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n966\t| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n967\t| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"e54519ff-b50e-4f99-98ee-6d3912db3e9e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:37:06.482Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n**Plate Cam** (Mode 7): `m7.cam = { x: H.x + side, y: H.y + 72, h: 36, angle: −π/2, pitch: 9, fov: 46 }` (`TUNE.plateDist`,\n`plateHeight`, `plateFov`). `side` is 4 U toward the side away from the batter, so the zone is clear.\n- The Plate Cam setting has two choices: *Classic* (the default) and *Close* (dist 52, h 30, fov 42).\n- The sky is the park's `E.Backdrop`. The floor is the park TileMap.\n- Billboards each frame: pitcher, fielders, batter, catcher, base runners and on-deck hitter with `m7.rig`; light\n  towers and props with `m7.shape`; the fence, stand faces, crowd rows, foul poles and scoreboard with `m7.add` quads\n  and sprites (far to near); the ball and its shadow with `m7.add` (radius max(1.5, 1.6 · k) px, stitches, the trail of\n  a super).\n- The umpire isn't drawn in the Plate Cam (he'd fill the screen); his calls show as a cut-in portrait.\n- The catcher crouches below the zone, drawn at 85% opacity where he overlaps it.\n- Overlays: the zone (a 3×3 grid; the setting is Always / During the pitch / Off), the reticle or target marker, the\n  release ring, the crossing dot. These are drawn from `m7.project` of their world points.\n- On contact the camera shakes for 0.15 s.\n\n**Plate Cam → Broadcast Cam.** On fair contact or a pop-up: hit-stop 0.06 s (`game.freeze`), a white flash\n(`game.flash('#fff', .06)`), then a cut to the Broadcast Cam framing the plate. Within 0.3 s it takes up\n`cam.frame([ballShadow, ballDrawn, landingMarker, chaser], { margin: 48, zoom: [.5, 1.1] })` with `cam.smooth = .15`.\n- Fouls back over the screen stay in the Plate Cam (the ball shrinks up and away, then \"FOUL BALL\").\n- Pop-ups high over the infield go to the Broadcast Cam, zoomed in.\n\n**Broadcast Cam framing by play:**\n- the ball in the air: the ball, its marker and the chaser;\n- a fielder with the ball: him, the throw's target base and the lead runner;\n- runners only: the lead runner and the next base.\n\nZoom stays within 0.5–1.1 × `TUNE.fieldZoom`. A wide screen shows more of the park; an upright phone zooms in. After\nthe play, a cut back to the Plate Cam for the next pitch.\n\n**Moonshot Cam.** When the prediction says a fair ball will clear the fence by 10 U or more (or on any Batzooka hit):\n1. `game.timeScale = .4`; Mode 7 `m7.chase(ball, { back: 60, h: 20 })` looks along the flight over the fence;\n2. the landing in the stands, a target or the bay: crowd scramble, fireworks, confetti (Broadcast Cam at the landing\n   point);\n3. normal speed; the **trot** in the Broadcast Cam (the batter jogs the bases, high-fiving the coaches; ×2 speed;\n   skippable with ✕).\n\nThe whole sequence lasts about 4 s plus the trot.\n\n**Replays.** A ring buffer keeps the last 8 s of the world at 60 Hz: the ball, every body's x, y, z and facing, and each\nrig's update inputs. After a big play (home run, diving or wall catch, double play, bowl-over, Batzooka, robbed homer)\nthe replay runs at ×0.5 in the engine's `iso` view (yaw 45, a new angle on the play), with a \"REPLAY\" banner and a pulsing\ncorner, and the jumbotron shows it too. ✕ skips it. The \"Replays\" setting is On / Big plays only (default) / Off.\n\n**Cutscene cameras:** `game.tween` on a focus point and the zoom, run by the cutscene runner (§6.15).\n\n**Tests:** T-CAM-01..07 (the Plate Cam projects the zone fully on screen at 427×240 and 320×240 for both box sides;\nthe cut on contact; the framing keeps ball and chaser on screen across 200 seeded batted balls (from snapshots of their\nscreen positions); the Moonshot Cam trigger; the replay buffer replays positions exactly; skips work; timeScale is\nrestored).\n\n### 6.13 HUD, prompts and glyphs (`22-hud.js`, `01-input.js`)\n\n**Layout** for a TV at 10 feet, anchored to `r.W` and `r.H` with a 12-pixel safe margin (TV overscan), kept clear of\n`game.cornerArea()`:\n- **Scorebug**, top left (scale 1 text, the score at scale 2): AWAY and HOME abbreviations with runs; the inning with\n  ▲ or ▼; two out pips; the count as **B ●●○ S ●○**; a mini diamond with lit bases.\n- **Meters**, top right: the batting team's BATZOOKA gauge (a bat-shaped bar that fills with flame and blinks when\n  full); the turbo bar; the wind arrow with mph.\n- **Player card**, bottom left: a portrait (`rig.drawPortrait`), the name, one stat line (avg and HR this game) or the\n  pitcher's stamina bar.\n- **Prompts**, bottom center: 2–5 icon-and-verb pairs for the current context. An action not available now is greyed\n  with its reason (\"BATZOOKA 63%\").\n- **Radio box**: the announcers' lines with portraits, bottom left above the player card (2 lines, non-modal, auto\n  advance).\n- **Call stamps**, center top: STEE-RIKE!, BALL FOUR, FOUL BALL, SAFE!, YER OUT!, HOME RUN!, BATZOOKA!!!, DOUBLE PLAY!,\n  RHUBARB!. Drawn with `E.font.title`, scale 3–4, a 3-color gradient, a pop-in tween and shake.\n- **Pitch info** after each pitch: \"CURVE 71 MPH\" (supers show silly speeds: \"MEGA HEATER 131 MPH!!\").\n- **Radar** in the Broadcast Cam, top right under the meters: the diamond and outfield with fielders, runners and\n  the ball.\n- **Pitching select**: four pitch names around a diamond of face-button icons in their positions (△ top, ○ right,\n  ✕ bottom, □ left), plus \"R1 SUPER\"; the stamina bar. With the mouse: the wheel highlights a pitch, left click throws.\n- **Throw targets** in the Broadcast Cam: when a fielder holds the ball, each base shows a ring with its button's icon\n  (○ 1B, △ 2B, □ 3B, ✕ HOME). With the mouse the rings take hover (they grow) and clicks.\n\n**Rules:** the important information is at scale 2 or more (the 5×7 font at scale 1 is about 31 px tall on a 1080p\nTV); no screen shows more than about 7 items of text at once; information is never in color alone (shapes and icons\ntoo); every HUD piece hides during cutscenes and replays.\n\n**The glyph icon renderer** `BZ.glyph(g, label, x, y, size = 11)` draws the icon for the string `input.label(action)`\nreturns (the font cannot draw ✕ ○ □ △ in color):\n- PlayStation: a dark disc (#1b1d26) with a light rim and the symbol in its classic color: ✕ blue #7aa7ff,\n  ○ red #ff6b6b, □ pink #f08ad8, △ green #4fe0b0.\n- Xbox: a dark disc with a colored letter: A green #6cc04a, B red #e8584a, X blue #4a8ef0, Y yellow #f4c430.\n- Shoulders and triggers (L1, R1, L2, R2, LB, RB, LT, RT): rounded pills with the text.\n- Options, Create, Menu, View: small pills. L3, R3, LS, RS: a stick ring with L or R.\n- D-pad: a cross with the direction lit.\n- Keyboard: a keycap (a light rounded rectangle with dark text; wide for Space, Shift, Enter).\n- Mouse: a mouse outline with the left or right half filled, or the wheel lit.\n\n**The prompt builder** `BZ.prompts(ctx)` → `[[action, verb, enabled, why]]`, drawn as icon + verb.\n- Labels follow `game.input.lastDevice`: for a gamepad, the `glyphs` family (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting);\n  for the keyboard, keys; for the mouse, the mouse buttons where the action has one and keys for the rest.\n- Each player's prompts use their own input (`p.input.label`).\n\n**Tests:** T-HUD-01..08 (`label` → icon type for every action on each device and family; the setting switches the\nfamily; prompts change with `lastDevice` within one step; nothing in the HUD overlaps the corner area or goes outside\nthe safe margins at 320×240, 427×240 and 320×690; greyed prompts give a reason).\n\n### 6.14 Input and controls (`01-input.js`)\n\n**One preset**, positional, passed as `new E.Game({ input: BZ.KEYS })` and on every scene. Actions are named by the\nbutton's position because the same button does a different job in each context (swing, fastball, throw home, punch).\nThat also keeps rebinding conflict-free. The keyboard's I J K L cluster sits like the face buttons.\n\n| Action | DualSense (standard mapping) | Xbox glyph | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `south` | ✕ (Pad0) | A | K, Space | left button |\n| `east` | ○ (Pad1) | B | L | – |\n| `west` | □ (Pad2) | X | J | right button |\n| `north` | △ (Pad3) | Y | I | – |\n| `l1` | L1 (Pad4) | LB | Q | – |\n| `r1` | R1 (Pad5) | RB | E | – |\n| `l2` | L2 (Pad6) | LT | Left Shift | – |\n| `r2` | R2 (Pad7) | RT | C, Left Ctrl | – |\n| `view` | R3 (Pad11) | RS | V | – |\n| `up/down/left/right` | left stick, D-pad | | W A S D, arrows | – |\n| `confirm` | ✕ | A | Enter, Space, K | click a menu item |\n| `cancel` | ○ | B | Escape, Backspace, L | – |\n| `pause` / `start` | Options (Pad9) | Menu | Escape, P / Enter | – |\n| the reticle and pitch target | left stick | | W A S D, arrows | the pointer (absolute) |\n| pitch type | face buttons | | K L J I | wheel to pick, left button to throw |\n\n**What each button does, by context** (prompts and the Playbook show this per device):\n\n| Context | ✕ south | ○ east | □ west | △ north | L1 | R1 | L2 | R2 | stick or mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Batting | contact swing | – | power swing | bunt (hold) | steal: hold, plus a base button | arm the BATZOOKA | – | – | aim the reticle |\n| Pitching | heater | breaking ball | off-speed | signature | pickoff: hold, plus a base button | super pitch | – | – | aim; aftertouch in flight |\n| Fielding (holding the ball) | throw home | throw to 1B | throw to 3B | throw to 2B | switch fielder | Laser Arm (plus a base button) | turbo | – | run; click a base to throw |\n| Fielding (chasing) | – | – | – | – | switch fielder | jump / wall climb | turbo | dive | run |\n| Running | headfirst dive (near a base) | (base select) | (base select) | (base select) | all back (or one, with a base button) | all advance (or one, with a base button) | turbo; with R1, Afterburners | – | mouse: left-click a base to send, right-click to bring back |\n| Rhubarb | punch combo | dodge | shove | grab and toss | – | – | – | – | move |\n| Menus | select | back | – | – | previous tab | next tab | – | – | hover focuses, click selects |\n\n- **Base buttons:** a face button picks a base by its position (○ first, △ second, □ third, ✕ home) in three cases:\n  when your fielder holds the ball (a throw), while L1 is held (a steal, a pickoff, sending one runner back), and while\n  R1 is held (sending one runner, or a Laser Arm throw). Otherwise the face buttons do their usual jobs.\n- **Mouse:** the pointer aims the bat or the pitch (pointer → zone plane by inverse-interpolating the zone's 4\n  projected corners). In the Broadcast Cam, `game.mouseGround()` within 26 U of a base hovers it. Fielding and running\n  use `input.suppressCode('Mouse0')` / `'Mouse2'` unless the click lands on a base, so a stray click never throws home.\n  The wheel is the game's in a match (`camera: { zoom: false }` on the match scene).\n- **Device tracking:** `lastDevice` switches on use; prompts follow it the next step. Moving the mouse counts as mouse\n  use.\n- **AI Hub rule:** in a session, Options is ignored while Create (Pad8) is held (the hub's bar).\n- **Local players 2–4** get their own `game.players[i]` inputs: a gamepad each, or half a keyboard each\n  (`E.Input.halfKeys`). The mouse belongs to player 1.\n- **Rumble:** contact (strength by Q), catches, collisions, Batzooka (strong), Rhubarb hits (`input.rumble`; vibration\n  setting).\n\n**Tests:** T-IN-01..10 (each context routes the right action; a press in a menu doesn't leak into play\n(`consumeAll`); the mouse mapping onto the zone; a base click throws and a stray click doesn't; the wheel picks pitch\ntypes; Create plus Options in a session is ignored; half-keyboard players; rebinding a positional action changes all\nof its contexts' prompts).\n\n### 6.15 Commentary, writing and babble voices (`23-talk.js`, `02-audio.js`)\n\n**The cast's voices:** HAP HARRIGAN (play-by-play; warm, quick, hyperbolic); \"BIG\" LOU BOMBECK (color; a retired\nslugger with malapropisms and tall tales); GLORIA VOX (the PA; slow, echoing); UMPIRE BARB HAMMERSMITH (calls with\ngusto); and in the Tour, the captains, GRANDPA IKE and MORTIMER MEGAMAX.\n\n**The commentary director:**\n- It listens to the bus and picks a line from the pool for the event's tag (Appendix C, §7.9).\n- Lines carry priority: 3 for a home run, Batzooka, Rhubarb, walk-off or robbery; 2 for outs, hits and steals; 1 for\n  routine plays. A higher priority line interrupts.\n- At most one line every 2.5 s. The same line isn't repeated within 20 lines.\n- Variables: `{batter} {pitcher} {team} {dist} {mph} {park} {score}`.\n- Hap and Lou alternate on big plays (Hap calls it, Lou reacts).\n- Lines show in the radio box (`E.Dialog` with `modal: false`, `auto: 2.4`, portrait, alpha .85). The PA and the\n  umpire show as stamps plus their own voice.\n- The \"Commentary\" setting is On / Big plays only / Off.\n\n**Writing style.** It is the source's style: baseball radio broadcasting, turned up.\n- Short punchy sentences in the present tense, and one image per line (\"That ball had a map and a flashlight\").\n- Baseball slang: dinger, tater, moonshot, frozen rope, can of corn, chin music, painting the black, Uncle Charlie, the\n  heater, cheese, seeing-eye single, worm-burner, Texas leaguer, ducks on the pond, the hot corner.\n- Running gags: Lou's stories about 1979; the vendor who always sells out; the mascot's bad luck.\n- Lines are no longer than about 90 characters.\n- Never mean, no profanity, no real players or teams, and no real quotes.\n\n**Babble** (Animalese/Simlish-style voices, `BZ.babble`):\n- **Hooks:** for dialog, set `talk.voice = null` and poll `talk.shown` each update; for each newly typed letter of\n  `talk.pages[talk.i]`, play a syllable. Stamps and the PA call `BZ.babble.say(text, voice)` straight.\n- **Syllables:** each letter a–z plays one note.\n  - Pitch = base × 2^((map[letter] + contour) / 12). Vowels are a 0, e +4, i +7, o +2, u −3, y +5; consonants a small\n    offset from a hash.\n  - Length = 1 / rate (× 1.3 for vowels, × 0.8 for consonants).\n  - Each note is `game.audio.sfx({ wave, freq, to: freq · 1.06, dur, vol, vib }, { vary: 0 })`. s, z and x add a\n    10-ms noise burst.\n- **Spaces and punctuation:** a space is a 40-ms gap; `.` `,` `!` `?` are longer pauses. After `!` the next 4 notes go\n  +3 semitones; `?` rises at the end. A word in capitals is louder and slower.\n- **Voice profiles:**\n\n| Voice | Wave | Base Hz | Rate (letters/s) | Other |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Hap | pulse | 190 | 20 | range 7 |\n| Lou | square | 120 | 15 | vib [6, .02] (gravel) |\n| Gloria (PA) | triangle | 150 | 9 | an echo copy at +120 ms, 40% volume |\n| Umpire Barb | square | 260 | 14 | long vowels held with vibrato (\"STEE-RIIIKE\") |\n| Dot | pulse12 | 300 | 22 | |\n| Ike | triangle | 170 | 12 | vib [5, .04] (shaky) |","numLines":200,"startLine":768,"totalLines":2120}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01K1xPEMPuU1gpqq6syeeepP","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
