{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2nmEuiSpDn11xdyTtN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01DWgFCbRU8Z3E3KojFTqcnp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":968,"limit":200},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6913,"cache_read_input_tokens":458888,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":6913},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"46838b6f-50cf-476a-a416-30ca7a25b27c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:37:15.368Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2nkzmtwPLppPMwAbbo","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01DWgFCbRU8Z3E3KojFTqcnp":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","offset":968,"limit":200}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DWgFCbRU8Z3E3KojFTqcnp","type":"tool_result","content":"968\t| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n969\t| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n970\t| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n971\t| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n972\t\n973\t- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n974\t- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n975\t\n976\t**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n977\t`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n978\t`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n979\t✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n980\t\n981\t**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\n982\tno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n983\t\n984\t### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n985\t\n986\t**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\n987\tno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\n988\tapart with a slow vibrato.\n989\t\n990\t| Song | Mood and makeup |\n991\t|---|---|\n992\t| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n993\t| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n994\t| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n995\t| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n996\t| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n997\t| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n998\t| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n999\t| `training` | light and bouncy |\n1000\t| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n1001\t| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n1002\t\n1003\t**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\n1004\tvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n1005\t\n1006\t**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n1007\t- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n1008\t- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n1009\t- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n1010\t- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n1011\t- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n1012\t- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n1013\t- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n1014\t- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n1015\t  engine's `explode`.\n1016\t- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n1017\t- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n1018\t- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n1019\t\n1020\t**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\n1021\twarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n1022\t\n1023\t### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n1024\t\n1025\t- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n1026\t  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n1027\t- **Behavior:**\n1028\t  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n1029\t  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n1030\t  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n1031\t  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n1032\t- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n1033\t  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n1034\t- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n1035\t  the between-innings shows.\n1036\t- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n1037\t- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n1038\t  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n1039\t  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n1040\t  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n1041\t  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n1042\t- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n1043\t\n1044\t**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\n1045\thand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n1046\t\n1047\t### 6.18 Game modes\n1048\t\n1049\t- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n1050\t  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n1051\t  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n1052\t    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n1053\t  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n1054\t    reward item and the next pin.\n1055\t  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n1056\t- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n1057\t  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n1058\t- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n1059\t  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n1060\t  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n1061\t  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n1062\t    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n1063\t  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n1064\t  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n1065\t  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n1066\t- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n1067\t  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n1068\t  be replayed.\n1069\t\n1070\t### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n1071\t\n1072\t- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n1073\t  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n1074\t- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n1075\t  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n1076\t- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n1077\t  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n1078\t- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n1079\t  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n1080\t  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n1081\t    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n1082\t  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n1083\t  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n1084\t\n1085\t### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n1086\t\n1087\t- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n1088\t  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n1089\t- **Sides:**\n1090\t  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n1091\t  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n1092\t  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n1093\t- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n1094\t  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n1095\t  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n1096\t  - With one human, they do everything.\n1097\t- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n1098\t- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n1099\t  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n1100\t  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n1101\t- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n1102\t\n1103\t**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\n1104\tplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n1105\t\n1106\t### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n1107\t\n1108\t**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\n1109\tsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\n1110\ttheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\n1111\tGhosts never affect your round.\n1112\t\n1113\tWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n1114\t(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\n1115\tgame stays local (§6.20).\n1116\t\n1117\t**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n1118\t```html\n1119\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n1120\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n1121\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1122\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1123\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n1124\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n1125\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n1126\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/K/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n1127\t```\n1128\tThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n1129\t`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n1130\t\n1131\t**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1132\t1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n1133\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n1134\t   normally to the title.\n1135\t3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n1136\t   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n1137\t4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n1138\t   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n1139\t   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n1140\t     check;\n1141\t   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n1142\t   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n1143\t   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n1144\t     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n1145\t   \n1146\t   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n1147\t   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n1148\t   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n1149\t5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n1150\t   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()`.\n1151\t6. **Reporting:**\n1152\t   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n1153\t     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n1154\t   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n1155\t     **once**.\n1156\t   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n1157\t7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n1158\t   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n1159\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n1160\t     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n1161\t   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n1162\t   \n1163\t   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n1164\t8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n1165\t   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n1166\t   are canvas text, never HTML.\n1167\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"d51158ef-a636-4d6b-a993-357eb92e673f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:37:15.375Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"| Mortimer | saw | 140 | 16 | nasal |\n| ENFORCER-9000 | saw plus square | 90 | 18 | pitch quantized to a 3-note arpeggio |\n| skeletons | triangle | 220 | 18 | noise \"clacks\" |\n| other captains | | | | a distinct base and wave each (§7.1) |\n\n- **Volume:** the \"Voices\" slider (`settings.define`, 0–1, default 0.8).\n- **Subtitles:** all speech is gibberish, so the text is always on screen.\n\n**Cutscene runner** `BZ.cut(steps)` runs a `game.script` over data steps:\n`['say', who, text]`, `['walk', who, x, y]`, `['pose', who, pose, expr]`, `['face', who, angle]`, `['cam', x, y, zoom, secs]`,\n`['sfx', name]`, `['music', song]`, `['fx', kind, x, y]`, `['wait', s]`, `['spawn', ...]`, `['call', fn]`.\n✕ advances a line; holding ○ for 1 s skips the scene (a ring fills around the icon).\n\n**Tests:** T-TALK-01..07 (every event tag has at least 6 lines; the rate limit; priority interrupts; variables fill in;\nno line over 90 characters; babble plays one syllable per letter, deterministically; the skip-hold works).\n\n### 6.16 Music and sound effects (`02-audio.js`, `56-songs.js`)\n\n**Music**, in the engine's song notation (`{ bpm, steps, tracks: [{ name, wave, vol, notes, on }] }`). All original:\nno real anthems, and not even public-domain ballpark tunes. The ballpark **organ** is square + triangle an octave\napart with a slow vibrato.\n\n| Song | Mood and makeup |\n|---|---|\n| `title` \"Batzooka!\" | 140 bpm; organ riff + chip rock bass + drums; a rocket-whoosh sting on the logo |\n| `menu` \"Clubhouse Shuffle\" | laid-back organ shuffle, 100 bpm |\n| `tourmap` \"Barnstorm Boogie\" | road boogie, 120 bpm |\n| one in-game loop per park | Kernel Field county-fair organ; Barnacle Bay 6/8 shanty; Caldera minor drums and saw bass; Tranquility spacey arpeggios; Boneyard spooky organ with a theremin-like vibrato sine; MegaDome synthwave. Ducked at the plate; layers named `base`, `hype` (the meter full), `drums` (runners in scoring position) |\n| `rally` | an original organ \"rally\" climb, played when the home team puts runners on |\n| `derby` | breakneck surf rock, 160 bpm |\n| `rhubarb` | a frantic chase, 180 bpm |\n| `training` | light and bouncy |\n| `ending` and `credits` | an anthemic reprise of the title |\n| `hubcard` | a title variation, quieter |\n\n**Stings** (`audio.sting`, on the bar): home run fanfare, strikeout sting, Batzooka ignition, inning change,\nvictory, defeat, between-innings show.\n\n**Sound effects** (`audio.define`; vary by quality):\n- **Bat:** \"tink\" (weak), \"crack\" (solid), \"KRAKOOM\" (Batzooka: noise burst + low boom + ring).\n- **Gloves:** mitt \"POP\", glove \"thup\".\n- **Pitches:** a whoosh pitched by speed; foul tip; thud in the dirt.\n- **Ball:** bounce, fence clang or thud, pole \"ping\".\n- **Crowd:** a filtered-noise murmur loop as a layer, swelling with the meter; cheer; groan; \"OOOH\"; boos; gasp.\n- **Ballpark:** organ stabs, the PA's echo.\n- **Players:** footsteps, slide, dive thump, climb scrabble, throw whoosh, collision crash.\n- **Spectacle:** fireworks (boom + crackle), the Batzooka charge (a rising saw and noise), a rocket trail loop, the\n  engine's `explode`.\n- **Parks:** gull caw, lava rumble, meteor whoosh, ghost \"boo\", drone buzz, boat horn.\n- **Interface:** meter-full ding, turbo whoosh, menu select, confirm and cancel.\n- **Rhubarb:** bonk, slide whistle, \"pow\".\n\n**Tests:** T-AUD-01..04 (every sound name used is defined; every scene sets its music; no unknown-song or unknown-sound\nwarnings in `game.errors` or the console; the Voices setting scales babble).\n\n### 6.17 Crowd and ballpark life (`24-crowd.js`)\n\n- **Crowd seats** on the stand tiles: 2–3 people a tile, drawn as in the sports kit: little bodies in shirts of the home\n  and away colors, skin tones, caps. Over 1,500 in a park, drawn `r.queue` with `every: 2`.\n- **Behavior:**\n  - They sway when idle, with an amplitude that follows the meter.\n  - They jump and cheer on big plays (by side), groan and sit on the other team's.\n  - They scramble where a home-run ball lands.\n  - **The wave** starts when the meter passes 70: a sine pulse running around the stands.\n- **Jumbotron** (a shape and a screen drawn by code): the batter's portrait and name; the replay; \"MAKE SOME NOISE!\",\n  \"BATZOOKA!\", \"SAFE?\"; the Derby leaders.\n- **Mascots:** a Blob mascot per home park dances on the dugout roof (squash on the beat, `audio.onBeat`) and stars in\n  the between-innings shows.\n- **Fireworks** (night parks) or **confetti cannons** (day parks) after home runs and wins.\n- **Between-innings shows**, one per half-inning (about 4 s, skippable with ✕):\n  - the **Mascot Race** (three mascots race the warning track; one always trips);\n  - **Dance Cam** (a fan dances on the jumbotron);\n  - the **T-Shirt Cannon** (shirts arc into the stands);\n  - the **Grounds Crew Shuffle** (rakers dance).\n- **Vendors:** babble shouts with text pops in the stands (\"PEANUTS!\", \"GET YER DOGS!\").\n\n**Tests:** T-CROWD-01..04 (reactions follow the events by side; the wave starts above 70; between-innings shows end and\nhand back; the jumbotron shows the current batter).\n\n### 6.18 Game modes\n\n- **TOUR** (`33-tour.js`), the story (§7.5): Spring Training is offered first (skippable), then five road games in\n  order, each with its cutscenes, its rival and its hazard, ending with the MegaDome boss game.\n  - The **Tour Map** is a stylized county map with the bus moving along a dotted road between six pins, where you\n    choose Play next stop, Clubhouse, Save, or Title.\n  - A **loss** gives a \"RAIN CHECK\" card: Retry (same stop) or Clubhouse first. A win gives Fan Bucks, the rival's\n    reward item and the next pin.\n  - Tour games last 3 innings (setting \"Tour game length\": Standard 3 / Quick 1) at the chosen difficulty.\n- **EXHIBITION**: Team Select (§6.24), then any unlocked park, innings 1/3/5/9, difficulty, and 1–4 players. Ends at\n  the results screen, then Rematch / Change teams / Title.\n- **BATZOOKA DERBY** (`31-derby.js`): 1–4 players take turns (hot seat). Each round is 2:00 or 10 outs, whichever\n  comes first (setting). The pitcher is the Pitch-O-Matic 3000 machine.\n  - **Outs:** any swing that isn't a home run, and any taken strike. Taken balls are free.\n  - **Score:** each home run's distance in feet, plus target bonuses: the blimp +500, the MEGA-TRON +300, drones +150,\n    the batter's-eye bullseye +200. Every 5th pitch is a **Golden Ball** worth ×2.\n  - Three home runs in a row fill the meter, for one Batzooka swing.\n  - At the end, initials for the **Moonshot Leaders** table (`E.Arcade` table `'derby'` and its letter wheel).\n  - The AI Hub round uses these rules (§6.21).\n- **SPRING TRAINING** (`32-training.js`): eight lessons (§7.6) taught by Grandpa Ike, with goals, prompts in the\n  player's own glyphs, slow-motion assists, and a stamp when passed. Ends with the \"House Rules\" card. Each lesson can\n  be replayed.\n\n### 6.19 Progression, unlocks and saves\n\n- **Fan Bucks (FB):** a win is worth 500, a loss 150. Bonuses: +50 a home run, +100 a Batzooka, +200 a shutout, plus\n  park targets (silo +300, drones +150 each, MEGA-TRON +300).\n- **Clubhouse upgrades** (team-wide, three tiers each; §7.8): Lumber (power), Glue Gloves (glove), Jet Cleats (speed),\n  Cannon Arms (arm), Hype Machine (meter gain). Each rival beaten gives one free tier of one upgrade.\n- **Unlocks:** Exhibition starts with every team and park except the MegaMax Mechanicals and the MegaDome, which\n  unlock when you reach the Tour's final. Dev mode can unlock all.\n- **Saves:** `game.saves`, three slots and an autosave. The data:\n  `{ v: 1, tour: { stop, beaten: [], wins, losses }, fb, upgrades, rewards, unlocked, training: { done: [] }, stats, derbyBest }`.\n  - Autosave after each Tour game and lesson; manual save on the Tour Map (`saves.canSave` only there); the title's\n    CONTINUE loads `saves.latest()`.\n  - Settings save themselves (the engine). Derby tables live in `E.store` (the arcade module).\n  - With storage blocked, or in an AI Hub session, everything plays on for the visit.\n\n### 6.20 Local multiplayer (1–4 on one machine)\n\n- Joining happens on Team Select (and Derby Setup): `game.allowJoin({ max: 4 })`. Press Start on a free gamepad or 2 on\n  the keyboard (half a keyboard each). Joined players' cards appear in their `E.PLAYER_COLORS`.\n- **Sides:**\n  - Each player moves their card left (away) or right (home), and can drop out.\n  - A side's first human is its **Captain**, the second its **Co-captain**. Sides with no humans are CPU.\n  - So the game covers 1P vs CPU, 2P versus, 2P co-op vs CPU, 2v1, 2v2 and 3v1.\n- **Duties when a side has two humans:**\n  - Defense: the captain pitches, the co-captain controls the fielders after contact.\n  - Offense: the captain bats, the co-captain runs the runners (steals, orders).\n  - With one human, they do everything.\n- **The Derby** takes turns, one player at a time, each with their own device or a shared one.\n- **On screen:** one shared screen. The Plate Cam is shared; the Broadcast Cam frames the action. Each human's\n  controlled fielder or runner gets a marker arrow in their player color. Prompts show per player at their side of the\n  screen (P1 bottom left, P2 bottom right; P3 and P4 just above).\n- **Pausing:** anyone's Options pauses (\"P2 PAUSED\"); a lost controller pauses and names its player (the engine).\n\n**Tests:** T-MP-01..05 (four players join with `addPlayer`; side assignment; duty routing; each prompt uses its own\nplayer's device; a removed player's side falls back to the CPU).\n\n### 6.21 AI Hub integration: the online Derby (`40-hub.js`)\n\n**The mode is `ghost`, 1–4 players.** Everyone plays the same Derby round on their own device, from the same pitch\nsequence, and sees the others as ghosts. The others' home runs fly through your sky as comets in their seat color, and\ntheir batters stand as translucent holograms in the on-deck circles and on the dugout rails, swinging when they swing.\nGhosts never affect your round.\n\nWhy not a shared-world head-to-head game: real batting timing does not survive 150 ms of latency and 50 ms of jitter\n(the hub's own test conditions), and a host-authoritative baseball world is a lot of risk for a demo. A head-to-head\ngame stays local (§6.20).\n\n**Page tags**, in `<head>`, after the client:\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Hit the most home-run feet in two minutes\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Aim: stick/WASD/mouse · Swing: ✕/K/click · Power: □/J/right-click\">\n```\nThe client `vendor/aihub-session.js` is pasted unmodified as the **first script in `<head>`, right after\n`<meta charset>`** (§12).\n\n**Flow when `AIHub.active`:**\n1. Boot skips the title, menus and attract mode. The boot steps run, then the `hubDerby` scene in its `loading` state.\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true })`. With `null`, the game goes on\n   normally to the title.\n3. Build the round: the MegaDome at night; the pitch sequence from `BZ.rng.deal = AIHub.rng('pitches')`; wind and\n   targets from `AIHub.rng('park')`. Then call **`AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press**.\n4. **Our own card** (`hubCard`; its look is the game's):\n   - the logo and \"BATZOOKA DERBY · ONLINE\";\n   - the players lined up as batters in their seat colors (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) with a READY\n     check;\n   - \"How to win: hit the most home-run feet in 2:00\";\n   - this player's controls as icons for their device;\n   - the time left from the `card` event (or \"Starting…\" when `starting`), \"Waiting for more time…\" when `paused`, and\n     \"You asked for more time\" when `asked`.\n   \n   ✕, Enter or a click on READY sends `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △, M or a click on MORE TIME sends\n   `AIHub.answer('more')`. The card **never calls `Math.random`**: no particles or weather, and sounds with `vary: 0`.\n   It has no menus and changes nothing.\n5. `start`: a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0`. Play begins at `AIHub.time() >= 0`, and the round clock is\n   `min(120000, info.limitMs − 5000) − AIHub.time()`.\n6. **Reporting:**\n   - Every second: `AIHub.progress({ score, label: '1,284 ft · 4 HR · 3 outs' })`, from 0 onward. (The harness only\n     presses arrows and WASD, so progress must flow without hits.)\n   - When the round ends (clock or 10 outs): `AIHub.result({ score, stats: { 'Home runs': n, 'Longest': '512 ft', Outs: k }, awards: [{ title: 'Moonshot', detail: '512 ft' }] })`,\n     **once**.\n   - On `time-up`: report at once if not yet reported.\n7. **Ghost traffic**, at most 20 messages a second and well under 16 KB:\n   - 10 Hz unreliable `{ t: 'g', area: 'derby', s: swingPhase, rx, rz, sc: score, o: outs }`;\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'hit', n, p: [x, y, z], v: [vx, vy, vz], spin, kind, dist, bz }` on each batted ball, which\n     receivers simulate locally with the same physics (cosmetic);\n   - a reliable `{ t: 'done', score }` at the end.\n   \n   `leave` removes that ghost; `message` from unknown ids is ignored.\n8. **`end`:** stop input (an `ended` flag: the scene ignores input and the game sends nothing), stop the sound\n   (`audio.music(null)` and the effects muted), and show \"ROUND OVER\" with the standings drawn on the canvas. Names\n   are canvas text, never HTML.\n","numLines":200,"startLine":968,"totalLines":2120}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01DWgFCbRU8Z3E3KojFTqcnp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
