{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01622LVhG7uNpBnBbyKznW8x","type":"tool_result","content":"1812\t**M3: Fielding, running and the full game.**\n1813\t- Fielders (assignment, chase, catch, throw, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns); baserunners (forces, tag-ups, orders,\n1814\t  steals, pickoffs, slides).\n1815\t- The full rules engine (innings, end of game, extra innings, mercy, errors).\n1816\t- The human fielding and running controls, mouse included; the CPU brains; line scores and the results screen.\n1817\t- **Done when:** T-RULES, T-FIELD, T-RUN and T-AI pass (CPU vs CPU sims in range), and a human vs CPU 3-inning game\n1818\t  plays from start to finish.\n1819\t\n1820\t**M4: The over-the-top layer.**\n1821\t- The meter and all supers; turbo; dive, jump and wall climb; headfirst dives; the bowl-over.\n1822\t- The Moonshot Cam, fireworks and the trot; replays; the crowd, hype and the wave; the jumbotron; mascots;\n1823\t  between-innings shows; the Rhubarb.\n1824\t- The commentary director with the babble voices; all music and sound effects.\n1825\t- **Done when:** T-SUPER, T-RHU, T-CAM, T-TALK, T-AUD and T-CROWD pass, and filmstrips of the swing, pitch, dive,\n1826\t  slide, wall climb, umpire calls and Rhubarb punches look clean (wind-up, strike, settle).\n1827\t\n1828\t**M5: The front end, input polish, dev mode and saves.**\n1829\t- Title (with its mode menu), Team Select with joining (1–4), results, Playbook, the pause items, About/Credits (with\n1830\t  provisional feedback text, rewritten in M8).\n1831\t- Our settings; the glyph renderer and prompts (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting, keyboard, mouse); mouse\n1832\t  hover and clicks on every screen and in the world.\n1833\t- The touch-only card; dev mode (all six tabs, pinned sliders, the gallery); saves.\n1834\t- **Done when:** T-UI, T-IN, T-HUD, T-DEV, T-MP and T-TOUCH pass, and screenshots of the prompts for a PlayStation pad,\n1835\t  an Xbox setting, the keyboard and the mouse look right.\n1836\t\n1837\t**M6: Content.**\n1838\t- All six teams and rosters with the captains' signatures and team supers; the six parks with their looks, lights and\n1839\t  hazards; the boss rules.\n1840\t- The Tour (map, clubhouse, rewards, every cutscene, the ending and credits roll); Spring Training's eight lessons.\n1841\t- All line pools.\n1842\t- **Done when:** T-HAZ, T-TOUR and T-TRAIN pass, the playthrough test passes, and screenshots of every park in both\n1843\t  main cameras look like their descriptions.\n1844\t\n1845\t**M7: The attract mode.**\n1846\t- The director and the ten segments, the idle detection, exiting on input, touch-only compatibility.\n1847\t- **Done when:** T-ATT passes and a filmed loop (screenshots every 10 s across one loop) shows every segment.\n1848\t\n1849\t**M8: Polish, verify and ship.**\n1850\t- **Balance:** sims and slider sessions; then write the tuned defaults into `TUNE` (the \"reset to defaults\" values).\n1851\t- **Look:** act on every `check.mjs` look note; check every park and screen at TV size.\n1852\t- **Animation:** a filmstrip pass.\n1853\t- **Writing:** a pass over every line for tone, length and repetition.\n1854\t- **Feedback:** write the final About text and the four feedback sections.\n1855\t- **Ship:** grep for leftovers (§12); `exit-interview.json`; the final bundle; every test, the browser checks and the\n1856\t  hub harness green.\n1857\t\n1858\t---\n1859\t\n1860\t## 10. Risks and how to take them down\n1861\t\n1862\t| # | Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk early | Fallback |\n1863\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1864\t| R1 | The Plate Cam (Mode 7) doesn't read: the batter hides the zone, near rigs look odd, walls sort wrong | Mode 7's billboards are mostly used for karts, not near-camera people | Prototype on day one of M1, with screenshots at both box sides; every camera number in TUNE; the batter offset to the side, the camera shifted 4 U the other way; the catcher at 85% opacity | A custom low `E.View` (pitch 18) from behind the plate, with the ball's size scaled by its distance and drawn by hand (an orthographic view) |\n1865\t| R2 | Batting isn't fun or fair | Timing, aim and outcome must feel causal | A deterministic pitch path with a known crossing; the dev timing bar and contact readout; timing feedback text; unit tests of the contact mapping; sims of outcome rates; assists by difficulty | Widen the windows and sweet spot by TUNE; an aim assist that pulls the reticle 30% toward the ball |\n1866\t| R3 | Rules edge cases (forces, tag-ups, third-out timing) | Baseball's rules are subtle | A pure rules module with a table-driven test of 24+ situations; explicit lists of kept and dropped rules | Simplify the case (for example, auto-return runners on caught flies) and say so in House Rules |\n1867\t| R4 | Fielding AI looks dumb: two fielders on one ball, nobody covering | Roles depend on the situation | A role table by ball direction and runners; a test of coverage in each situation; one chaser at a time | Auto-assign the cover men by the nearest open base |\n1868\t| R5 | Fly balls leave the screen in the Broadcast Cam | High apexes | The display height squash, the landing marker, `cam.frame` zooming out to 0.5 | A small picture-in-picture \"ball cam\" in a corner showing the ball against the sky |\n1869\t| R6 | Mocap clips fit badly (the bat's direction, long takes, mirroring) | CMU takes are raw | The window finder; review in the gallery with filmstrips; procedural fallbacks already written | Use the procedural moves for that motion |\n1870\t| R7 | The hub protocol fails in the single file | Ordering of scripts, the card, cadence | Build it in M2, run the real harness then and keep it in `--hub`; the mock in headless tests; follow §6.21 to the letter | Parallel mode (drop the ghost traffic and change one meta), if ghosts are the failing check |\n1871\t| R8 | The bundle breaks: `</script>` inside inlined code, an external request, the file:// origin | Inlining 1.3 MB of code | The build scans for `</script` and `<!--` in the inlined code and fails if it finds them; `check.mjs` lists external requests (there must be none); test both file:// and served | Split the inlined code into several `<script>` blocks |\n1872\t| R9 | PlayStation symbols missing from the font | The 5×7 font is ASCII plus a few symbols | The icon renderer, plus the four glyphs added to the font; T-HUD checks every label | – |\n1873\t| R10 | Scope: many systems and much content | A demo with a whole Tour | Strict milestones; content as data; the playthrough test catches regressions | Quick Tour games (1 inning) by default if time runs short (the setting exists anyway) |\n1874\t| R11 | The attract director is brittle with forced outcomes | Scripted events can miss | A time limit per segment and moving on by itself; forced outcomes through `game.debug.force` | Skip the segment and log it (a test fails if any segment is skipped) |\n1875\t| R12 | Determinism drifts (tests flake) | Cosmetic code using `Math.random` in the rules | The rng-stream rule; the proof hash twice per run (T-CORE) | – |\n1876\t| R13 | The shared machine: a full `/tmp`, ports, other agents | Seen while planning | `TMPDIR` in the dev folder; the server on 127.0.0.1 with port 0; stop only your own PIDs | – |\n1877\t\n1878\t---\n1879\t\n1880\t## 11. Testing\n1881\t\n1882\t### 11.1 One command\n1883\t\n1884\t`node tests/run.mjs` (also `npm test`) runs every headless suite and prints **only failures**, then one line:\n1885\t`212 passed, 0 failed (38.4 s)`. The exit code is 1 on any failure.\n1886\t- `--browser` adds the browser checks (§11.6), `--hub` the AI Hub harness (§11.7), `--full` the long balance sims\n1887\t  (§11.5); `--all` runs everything.\n1888\t- `--only <suite>` reruns one suite. Rerun a suite only after changing the code it covers.\n1889\t- Tests and tools play the game headless and read its state. Screenshots are only for what a script can't judge: the\n1890\t  look, framing and readability.\n1891\t- Per TASK.md, no subagents or workflows debate, review, judge or audit.\n1892\t\n1893\t**The shared machine:**\n1894\t- Prefer file:// and the headless tools.\n1895\t- The hub harness needs a server: `tools/serve.mjs` binds **127.0.0.1 on port 0** and prints the port the OS picked (or\n1896\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`). Fetch the page once to confirm it is this game (its `<title>` is\n1897\t  \"Batzooka Baseball\") before trusting a result.\n1898\t- Stop only the processes you started, by their PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds\n1899\t  a port.\n1900\t- Don't read files outside the dev folder and this folder, apart from the sources in §3.1.\n1901\t- If `/tmp` is full, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp` for Playwright.\n1902\t\n1903\t### 11.2 The headless harness and `game.debug`\n1904\t\n1905\t`tests/lib/load.mjs` makes a `vm` context with the stand-ins the kits' test uses (`window`, `addEventListener`,\n1906\t`location`), plus `navigator`, `matchMedia` and a fake `AIHub` when asked. It runs `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, the mocap\n1907\tfiles and `build/game.js` in it, and returns `{ E, game, BZ }`.\n1908\t- Tests drive the game with `game.advance(seconds)` (exact 1/120 s steps), `game.input.tap / hold`, `addPlayer` and\n1909\t  `input.feed` (mouse), and read `game.debug.snapshot()`.\n1910\t- The bots (§6.11) play real controls.\n1911\t- A determinism check (T-CORE-03) runs the same seeded half-inning twice and compares the hash of its snapshots.\n1912\t\n1913\t`game.debug` (Appendix D) has `snapshot`, `setup`, `force`, `tune`, `resetTune`, `events`, `bot`, `step`, `skip`,\n1914\t`warp` and `hubMock`.\n1915\t\n1916\t### 11.3 The test catalog\n1917\t\n1918\t| Suite | IDs | Covers |\n1919\t|---|---|---|\n1920\t| core | T-CORE-01..05 | registries; the bus; RNG streams (no `Math.random` in rules: wrap it with a counter during a sim, and it must stay 0 from rules code); determinism; TUNE meta complete (every key has a label, range and default) |\n1921\t| rules | T-RULES-01..24 | §6.1 |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"708825b7-ef23-4499-af9e-674001e2db5c","uuid":"9e7faeb1-0206-4bd1-a340-96d7c71e1551","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:39:07.425Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174819__xcfe199/work/PLAN.md","content":"**M3: Fielding, running and the full game.**\n- Fielders (assignment, chase, catch, throw, cutoffs, tags, forces, rundowns); baserunners (forces, tag-ups, orders,\n  steals, pickoffs, slides).\n- The full rules engine (innings, end of game, extra innings, mercy, errors).\n- The human fielding and running controls, mouse included; the CPU brains; line scores and the results screen.\n- **Done when:** T-RULES, T-FIELD, T-RUN and T-AI pass (CPU vs CPU sims in range), and a human vs CPU 3-inning game\n  plays from start to finish.\n\n**M4: The over-the-top layer.**\n- The meter and all supers; turbo; dive, jump and wall climb; headfirst dives; the bowl-over.\n- The Moonshot Cam, fireworks and the trot; replays; the crowd, hype and the wave; the jumbotron; mascots;\n  between-innings shows; the Rhubarb.\n- The commentary director with the babble voices; all music and sound effects.\n- **Done when:** T-SUPER, T-RHU, T-CAM, T-TALK, T-AUD and T-CROWD pass, and filmstrips of the swing, pitch, dive,\n  slide, wall climb, umpire calls and Rhubarb punches look clean (wind-up, strike, settle).\n\n**M5: The front end, input polish, dev mode and saves.**\n- Title (with its mode menu), Team Select with joining (1–4), results, Playbook, the pause items, About/Credits (with\n  provisional feedback text, rewritten in M8).\n- Our settings; the glyph renderer and prompts (PlayStation by default, Xbox by setting, keyboard, mouse); mouse\n  hover and clicks on every screen and in the world.\n- The touch-only card; dev mode (all six tabs, pinned sliders, the gallery); saves.\n- **Done when:** T-UI, T-IN, T-HUD, T-DEV, T-MP and T-TOUCH pass, and screenshots of the prompts for a PlayStation pad,\n  an Xbox setting, the keyboard and the mouse look right.\n\n**M6: Content.**\n- All six teams and rosters with the captains' signatures and team supers; the six parks with their looks, lights and\n  hazards; the boss rules.\n- The Tour (map, clubhouse, rewards, every cutscene, the ending and credits roll); Spring Training's eight lessons.\n- All line pools.\n- **Done when:** T-HAZ, T-TOUR and T-TRAIN pass, the playthrough test passes, and screenshots of every park in both\n  main cameras look like their descriptions.\n\n**M7: The attract mode.**\n- The director and the ten segments, the idle detection, exiting on input, touch-only compatibility.\n- **Done when:** T-ATT passes and a filmed loop (screenshots every 10 s across one loop) shows every segment.\n\n**M8: Polish, verify and ship.**\n- **Balance:** sims and slider sessions; then write the tuned defaults into `TUNE` (the \"reset to defaults\" values).\n- **Look:** act on every `check.mjs` look note; check every park and screen at TV size.\n- **Animation:** a filmstrip pass.\n- **Writing:** a pass over every line for tone, length and repetition.\n- **Feedback:** write the final About text and the four feedback sections.\n- **Ship:** grep for leftovers (§12); `exit-interview.json`; the final bundle; every test, the browser checks and the\n  hub harness green.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Risks and how to take them down\n\n| # | Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk early | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| R1 | The Plate Cam (Mode 7) doesn't read: the batter hides the zone, near rigs look odd, walls sort wrong | Mode 7's billboards are mostly used for karts, not near-camera people | Prototype on day one of M1, with screenshots at both box sides; every camera number in TUNE; the batter offset to the side, the camera shifted 4 U the other way; the catcher at 85% opacity | A custom low `E.View` (pitch 18) from behind the plate, with the ball's size scaled by its distance and drawn by hand (an orthographic view) |\n| R2 | Batting isn't fun or fair | Timing, aim and outcome must feel causal | A deterministic pitch path with a known crossing; the dev timing bar and contact readout; timing feedback text; unit tests of the contact mapping; sims of outcome rates; assists by difficulty | Widen the windows and sweet spot by TUNE; an aim assist that pulls the reticle 30% toward the ball |\n| R3 | Rules edge cases (forces, tag-ups, third-out timing) | Baseball's rules are subtle | A pure rules module with a table-driven test of 24+ situations; explicit lists of kept and dropped rules | Simplify the case (for example, auto-return runners on caught flies) and say so in House Rules |\n| R4 | Fielding AI looks dumb: two fielders on one ball, nobody covering | Roles depend on the situation | A role table by ball direction and runners; a test of coverage in each situation; one chaser at a time | Auto-assign the cover men by the nearest open base |\n| R5 | Fly balls leave the screen in the Broadcast Cam | High apexes | The display height squash, the landing marker, `cam.frame` zooming out to 0.5 | A small picture-in-picture \"ball cam\" in a corner showing the ball against the sky |\n| R6 | Mocap clips fit badly (the bat's direction, long takes, mirroring) | CMU takes are raw | The window finder; review in the gallery with filmstrips; procedural fallbacks already written | Use the procedural moves for that motion |\n| R7 | The hub protocol fails in the single file | Ordering of scripts, the card, cadence | Build it in M2, run the real harness then and keep it in `--hub`; the mock in headless tests; follow §6.21 to the letter | Parallel mode (drop the ghost traffic and change one meta), if ghosts are the failing check |\n| R8 | The bundle breaks: `</script>` inside inlined code, an external request, the file:// origin | Inlining 1.3 MB of code | The build scans for `</script` and `<!--` in the inlined code and fails if it finds them; `check.mjs` lists external requests (there must be none); test both file:// and served | Split the inlined code into several `<script>` blocks |\n| R9 | PlayStation symbols missing from the font | The 5×7 font is ASCII plus a few symbols | The icon renderer, plus the four glyphs added to the font; T-HUD checks every label | – |\n| R10 | Scope: many systems and much content | A demo with a whole Tour | Strict milestones; content as data; the playthrough test catches regressions | Quick Tour games (1 inning) by default if time runs short (the setting exists anyway) |\n| R11 | The attract director is brittle with forced outcomes | Scripted events can miss | A time limit per segment and moving on by itself; forced outcomes through `game.debug.force` | Skip the segment and log it (a test fails if any segment is skipped) |\n| R12 | Determinism drifts (tests flake) | Cosmetic code using `Math.random` in the rules | The rng-stream rule; the proof hash twice per run (T-CORE) | – |\n| R13 | The shared machine: a full `/tmp`, ports, other agents | Seen while planning | `TMPDIR` in the dev folder; the server on 127.0.0.1 with port 0; stop only your own PIDs | – |\n\n---\n\n## 11. Testing\n\n### 11.1 One command\n\n`node tests/run.mjs` (also `npm test`) runs every headless suite and prints **only failures**, then one line:\n`212 passed, 0 failed (38.4 s)`. The exit code is 1 on any failure.\n- `--browser` adds the browser checks (§11.6), `--hub` the AI Hub harness (§11.7), `--full` the long balance sims\n  (§11.5); `--all` runs everything.\n- `--only <suite>` reruns one suite. Rerun a suite only after changing the code it covers.\n- Tests and tools play the game headless and read its state. Screenshots are only for what a script can't judge: the\n  look, framing and readability.\n- Per TASK.md, no subagents or workflows debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n**The shared machine:**\n- Prefer file:// and the headless tools.\n- The hub harness needs a server: `tools/serve.mjs` binds **127.0.0.1 on port 0** and prints the port the OS picked (or\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1`). Fetch the page once to confirm it is this game (its `<title>` is\n  \"Batzooka Baseball\") before trusting a result.\n- Stop only the processes you started, by their PID. Never use `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds\n  a port.\n- Don't read files outside the dev folder and this folder, apart from the sources in §3.1.\n- If `/tmp` is full, set `TMPDIR=<dev>/.tmp` for Playwright.\n\n### 11.2 The headless harness and `game.debug`\n\n`tests/lib/load.mjs` makes a `vm` context with the stand-ins the kits' test uses (`window`, `addEventListener`,\n`location`), plus `navigator`, `matchMedia` and a fake `AIHub` when asked. It runs `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, the mocap\nfiles and `build/game.js` in it, and returns `{ E, game, BZ }`.\n- Tests drive the game with `game.advance(seconds)` (exact 1/120 s steps), `game.input.tap / hold`, `addPlayer` and\n  `input.feed` (mouse), and read `game.debug.snapshot()`.\n- The bots (§6.11) play real controls.\n- A determinism check (T-CORE-03) runs the same seeded half-inning twice and compares the hash of its snapshots.\n\n`game.debug` (Appendix D) has `snapshot`, `setup`, `force`, `tune`, `resetTune`, `events`, `bot`, `step`, `skip`,\n`warp` and `hubMock`.\n\n### 11.3 The test catalog\n\n| Suite | IDs | Covers |\n|---|---|---|\n| core | T-CORE-01..05 | registries; the bus; RNG streams (no `Math.random` in rules: wrap it with a counter during a sim, and it must stay 0 from rules code); determinism; TUNE meta complete (every key has a label, range and default) |\n| rules | T-RULES-01..24 | §6.1 |","numLines":110,"startLine":1812,"totalLines":2125}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01622LVhG7uNpBnBbyKznW8x","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
